PathwayData.put("data/grammar_vocabulary/units/L11U5.json",{"strand":"grammar_vocabulary","level":11,"cefr":"C1","unit":5,"theme":"Environment and Responsibility","essential_question":"Who is responsible for protecting the environment: governments, companies or individuals?","lessons":[{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS1","title":"A Long-Term Tree-Planting Program That Needs Little Water: Complex Noun Phrases","objectives":["Students will be able to build complex noun phrases with modifiers before and after the head noun.","Students will be able to combine several pieces of information into one precise noun phrase and make the verb agree with the head.","I can write \"a long-term tree-planting program\".","I can write \"The amount of plastic produced every year is enormous.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من بناء أشباه جمل اسمية معقدة بمعدِّلات قبل الاسم الرئيس وبعده.","سيتمكن الطلاب من دمج عدة معلومات في شبه جملة اسمية واحدة دقيقة وجعل الفعل يتفق مع الاسم الرئيس.","أستطيع أن أكتب a long-term tree-planting program.","أستطيع أن أكتب The amount of plastic produced every year is enormous."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"desertification","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˌzɝtəfəˈkeɪʃən/","definition":"the process by which fertile land becomes desert","arabic":"التصحّر","example":"Overgrazing has contributed to desertification.","image_id":"GV-L11-U5-LS1-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS1-W01"},{"word":"mitigate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈmɪtəˌɡeɪt/","definition":"to make something bad less serious","arabic":"يخفّف من","example":"Planting trees can help mitigate climate change.","image_id":"GV-L11-U5-LS1-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS1-W02"},{"word":"offset","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɔfˈsɛt/","definition":"to balance one effect with an opposite one","arabic":"يعوّض / يوازن","example":"Some airlines offset their carbon emissions by funding forests.","image_id":"GV-L11-U5-LS1-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS1-W03"},{"word":"carbon footprint","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkɑrbən ˈfʊtˌprɪnt/","definition":"the amount of carbon dioxide produced by a person's or organization's activities","arabic":"البصمة الكربونية","example":"Flying increases your carbon footprint.","image_id":"GV-L11-U5-LS1-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS1-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"A noun phrase has a head noun and modifiers. Pre-modifiers come before it: determiner + (adverb) + adjective(s) + noun modifier + HEAD: a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign. Nouns can modify nouns: water consumption, a solar plant, carbon emissions. Compound adjectives use hyphens: salt-tolerant, long-term, large-scale, low-cost. A number + noun modifier stays singular: a three-year plan (NOT (not: three-years)).\nPost-modifiers come after the head: a prepositional phrase (trees on the coast), a relative clause (trees that need little water), an -ing phrase (volunteers carrying seedlings = who are carrying), an -ed phrase (water used for irrigation = that is used), a to-infinitive (a plan to cut emissions). Long noun phrases pack information: The program plants trees. The trees are native. They need little water. → The program plants native trees that need little water.\nThe verb agrees with the head noun, not with the nearest noun: The amount of plastic produced every year is enormous. The number of trees planted along the coasts has grown. Much of the food bought by households ends up in landfill. Don't overload: if a noun phrase has more than about three modifiers before the head, move some after it.\npre: determiner + adverb + adjective + noun modifier + HEAD\nnumber + noun modifier → singular (a three-year plan)\npost: prepositional phrase • relative clause • -ing / -ed phrase • to-infinitive\nthe verb agrees with the head noun","examples":["A long-term tree-planting program was launched in 2021.","Native species that need little water were chosen.","Treated wastewater from nearby cities is used for irrigation.","Volunteers carrying thousands of seedlings planted mangroves.","The water used for irrigation is recycled.","The Kingdom has a plan to reach net zero by 2060.","A three-year study of desert plants found surprising results.","The amount of plastic produced every year is enormous."],"common_errors":["✗ a three-years study → ✓ a three-year study (Number + noun modifier → singular.)","✗ The amount of plastic produced every year are enormous. → ✓ The amount of plastic produced every year is enormous. (Head noun amount.)","✗ The trees which chosen for the program are native. → ✓ The trees chosen for the program are native. (Reduced relative: no which.)","✗ a program tree-planting long-term → ✓ a long-term tree-planting program (Modifiers before the head.)"],"arabic":"شبه الجملة الاسمية لها اسم رئيس ومعدِّلات: قبله (المحدد + الظرف + الصفة + الاسم الواصف): a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign.\nالأسماء قد تصف أسماء أخرى: water consumption، والصفات المركبة تأخذ شَرطة: salt-tolerant، والعدد + الاسم الواصف يبقى مفردًا: a three-year plan.\nبعد الاسم: شبه جملة جرية، أو جملة وصل، أو عبارة -ing أو -ed، أو مصدر: water used for irrigation، a plan to cut emissions.\nالفعل يتفق مع الاسم الرئيس: The amount of plastic produced every year is enormous."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “A Long-Term Tree-Planting Program That Needs Little Water: Complex Noun Phrases”. Let’s learn pre-modification (determiner + adverb + adjective + noun modifier + head: a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign; noun + noun: water consumption; compound adjectives: salt-tolerant, long-term, large-scale; singular nouns as modifiers: a three-year plan); post-modification (prepositional phrases, relative clauses, -ing and -ed participle phrases, to-infinitives); combining information into one noun phrase; agreement with the head noun together.","on_screen":"GV-L11-U5-LS1 | A Long-Term Tree-Planting Program That Needs Little Water: Complex Noun Phrases","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Listen to the start of “Greening the Desert”: In 2021, Saudi Arabia launched an ambitious national program to restore its natural environment: the Saudi Green Initiative. One of its most striking goals is to plant ten billion trees across the Kingdom in the coming decades, an effort that would help rehabilitate around forty million hectares of degraded land. Planting trees in an arid country is not simple. The trees chosen for the program are mostly native species that need little water, and many are irrigated with treated wastewater from nearby cities. Look at the green words.","on_screen":"In 2021, Saudi Arabia launched an ambitious national program to restore its natural environment: the Saudi Green Initiative. One of its most striking goals is to plant ten billion trees across the Kingdom in the coming decades, an effort that would help rehabilitate around forty million hectares of degraded land. Planting trees in an arid country is not simple. The trees chosen for the program are mostly native species that need little water, and many are irrigated with treated wastewater from nearby cities.","visual":"Image GV-L11-U5-LS1-IMG01 animates gently; target words glow Leaf Green as they are spoken."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"A noun phrase has a head noun and modifiers. Pre-modifiers come before it: determiner + (adverb) + adjective(s) + noun modifier + HEAD: a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign. Nouns can modify nouns: water consumption, a solar plant, carbon emissions. Compound adjectives use hyphens: salt-tolerant, long-term, large-scale, low-cost. A number + noun modifier stays singular: a three-year plan (NOT (not: three-years)). Post-modifiers come after the head: a prepositional phrase (trees on the coast), a relative clause (trees that need little water), an -ing phrase (volunteers carrying seedlings = who are carrying), an -ed phrase (water used for irrigation = that is used), a to-infinitive (a plan to cut emissions). Long noun phrases pack information: The program plants trees. The trees are native. They need little water. → The program plants native trees that need little water.","on_screen":"pre: determiner + adverb + adjective + noun modifier + HEAD • number + noun modifier → singular (a three-year plan) • post: prepositional phrase • relative clause • -ing / -ed phrase • to-infinitive","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. A long-term tree-planting program was launched in 2021. Native species that need little water were chosen. Treated wastewater from nearby cities is used for irrigation.","on_screen":"A long-term tree-planting program was launched in 2021. / Native species that need little water were chosen. / Treated wastewater from nearby cities is used for irrigation.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “a three-years study.” That’s not right. We say: “a three-year study.” Number + noun modifier → singular.","on_screen":"✗ a three-years study   ✓ a three-year study","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"a three-years plan\" or \"a three-year plan\"? … Think! … *a three-year plan*. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"a long-term tree-planting program\".","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"a long-term tree-planting program\".","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: pre-modification (determiner + adverb + adjective + noun modifier + head: a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign; noun + noun: water consumption; compound adjectives: salt-tolerant, long-term, large-scale; singular nouns as modifiers: a three-year plan); post-modification (prepositional phrases, relative clauses, -ing and -ed participle phrases, to-infinitives); combining information into one noun phrase; agreement with the head noun.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","شبه الجملة الاسمية لها اسم رئيس ومعدِّلات: قبله (المحدد + الظرف + الصفة + الاسم الواصف): a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: a three-years study — الصحيح: a three-year study","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب a long-term tree-planting program."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Greening the Desert","body":"In 2021, Saudi Arabia launched an ambitious national program to restore its natural environment: the Saudi Green Initiative. One of its most striking goals is to plant ten billion trees across the Kingdom in the coming decades, an effort that would help rehabilitate around forty million hectares of degraded land.\nPlanting trees in an arid country is not simple. The trees chosen for the program are mostly native species that need little water, and many are irrigated with treated wastewater from nearby cities. Along the Red Sea and Gulf coasts, volunteers carrying thousands of seedlings have planted millions of mangroves, salt-tolerant trees that protect coastlines and provide homes for fish and birds.\nEnergy is another key area of change. The Kingdom has set a target of generating half of its electricity from renewable sources by 2030. Large-scale solar plants such as the one at Sudair can already supply power to well over a hundred thousand homes, and the country aims to reach net zero by 2060.\nIndividuals have a role to play as well. Water consumption per person in the Kingdom is among the highest in the world, and much of the food bought by households ends up in landfill. Simple, low-cost changes such as fixing leaking taps and planning meals can make a real difference.\nCritics point out that large tree-planting campaigns in dry regions can fail if seedlings are not cared for after they are planted. Supporters reply that careful long-term monitoring is part of the plan. What is certain is that the choices made by this generation will shape the land inherited by the next.","word_count":268,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS1-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"The Kingdom launched a ___ program in 2021.","options":["long-terms tree-plantings","long-term tree-planting","tree-planting long-term","long term-tree planting"],"answer":"long-term tree-planting","explanation":"Pre-modification (adjectives, noun modifiers, compounds).","explanation_ar":"التعديل القبلي."},{"q":"The researchers published a ___ study of desert plants.","options":["three years' long","three-yearly","three-year","three-years"],"answer":"three-year","explanation":"Number + singular noun modifier.","explanation_ar":"a three-year plan."},{"q":"The trees ___ for the program are mostly native species.","options":["chosen","which chosen","choosing","that chosen"],"answer":"chosen","explanation":"-ed participle phrase after the noun.","explanation_ar":"عبارة -ed."},{"q":"Volunteers ___ thousands of seedlings planted mangroves along the coast.","options":["carried","carry","who carrying","carrying"],"answer":"carrying","explanation":"-ing participle phrase after the noun.","explanation_ar":"عبارة -ing."},{"q":"The amount of plastic produced every year ___ enormous.","options":["are","were","is","have been"],"answer":"is","explanation":"Agreement with the head noun.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق مع الاسم الرئيس."},{"q":"Engineers have designed ______ that can reduce household water consumption by up to 30 percent.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["a water-saving low-cost devices","a device water-saving low-cost","a low-costs water-saving device","a low-cost water-saving device"],"answer":"a low-cost water-saving device","explanation":"Pre-modification (adjectives, noun modifiers, compounds).","explanation_ar":"التعديل القبلي."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS1-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Combine into one sentence with a noun phrase.","items":[{"q":"The program plants trees.  +  The trees are native and need little water. (native trees that…)","options":[],"answer":"The program plants native trees that need little water.","explanation":"Relative clause, preposition or to-infinitive after the noun.","explanation_ar":"التعديل البعدي."},{"q":"Water is used for irrigation.  +  It comes from treated wastewater. (The water used…)","options":[],"answer":"The water used for irrigation comes from treated wastewater.","explanation":"-ed participle phrase after the noun.","explanation_ar":"عبارة -ed."},{"q":"The Kingdom has a plan.  +  It aims to reach net zero by 2060. (a plan to…)","options":[],"answer":"The Kingdom has a plan to reach net zero by 2060.","explanation":"Relative clause, preposition or to-infinitive after the noun.","explanation_ar":"التعديل البعدي."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS1-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"The number of trees planted along the coast have grown rapidly.","options":[],"answer":"The number of trees planted along the coast has grown rapidly.","explanation":"have → has. Agreement with the head noun.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: have → has. الاتفاق مع الاسم الرئيس."},{"q":"The school launched a two-weeks recycling campaign.","options":[],"answer":"The school launched a two-week recycling campaign.","explanation":"two-weeks → two-week. Number + singular noun modifier.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: two-weeks → two-week. a three-year plan."},{"q":"The volunteers who planting mangroves were students.","options":[],"answer":"The volunteers planting mangroves were students.","explanation":"who planting → planting. -ing participle phrase after the noun.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: who planting → planting. عبارة -ing."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS1-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Build noun phrases.","items":[{"q":"Describe an environmental project with at least two pre-modifiers.","options":[],"answer":"Our school has a successful student-led recycling project.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses pre-modification (determiner + adverb + adjective + noun modifier + head: a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign; noun + noun: water consumption; compound adjectives: salt-tolerant, long-term, large-scale; singular nouns as modifiers: a three-year plan); post-modification (prepositional phrases, relative clauses, -ing and -ed participle phrases, to-infinitives); combining information into one noun phrase; agreement with the head noun correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Describe a place with a relative clause after the noun.","options":[],"answer":"Al-Ahsa is an oasis that produces millions of dates every year.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses pre-modification (determiner + adverb + adjective + noun modifier + head: a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign; noun + noun: water consumption; compound adjectives: salt-tolerant, long-term, large-scale; singular nouns as modifiers: a three-year plan); post-modification (prepositional phrases, relative clauses, -ing and -ed participle phrases, to-infinitives); combining information into one noun phrase; agreement with the head noun correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Use an -ing phrase after a noun.","options":[],"answer":"Families living near the coast have noticed more birds.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses pre-modification (determiner + adverb + adjective + noun modifier + head: a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign; noun + noun: water consumption; compound adjectives: salt-tolerant, long-term, large-scale; singular nouns as modifiers: a three-year plan); post-modification (prepositional phrases, relative clauses, -ing and -ed participle phrases, to-infinitives); combining information into one noun phrase; agreement with the head noun correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Use an -ed phrase after a noun.","options":[],"answer":"Plastic thrown into the sea harms turtles.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses pre-modification (determiner + adverb + adjective + noun modifier + head: a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign; noun + noun: water consumption; compound adjectives: salt-tolerant, long-term, large-scale; singular nouns as modifiers: a three-year plan); post-modification (prepositional phrases, relative clauses, -ing and -ed participle phrases, to-infinitives); combining information into one noun phrase; agreement with the head noun correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Use a to-infinitive after a noun.","options":[],"answer":"The city has a plan to plant trees along every main road.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses pre-modification (determiner + adverb + adjective + noun modifier + head: a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign; noun + noun: water consumption; compound adjectives: salt-tolerant, long-term, large-scale; singular nouns as modifiers: a three-year plan); post-modification (prepositional phrases, relative clauses, -ing and -ed participle phrases, to-infinitives); combining information into one noun phrase; agreement with the head noun correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS1-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: An environmental report.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 sentences about a green project with long noun phrases.","options":[],"answer":"A large-scale solar plant built near Riyadh supplies clean electricity. Native trees planted along the highway reduce dust.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses pre-modification (determiner + adverb + adjective + noun modifier + head: a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign; noun + noun: water consumption; compound adjectives: salt-tolerant, long-term, large-scale; singular nouns as modifiers: a three-year plan); post-modification (prepositional phrases, relative clauses, -ing and -ed participle phrases, to-infinitives); combining information into one noun phrase; agreement with the head noun correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Combine into one sentence: \"Some seedlings were planted last year. They need daily watering. They are on the school roof.\"","options":[],"answer":"The seedlings planted on the school roof last year need daily watering.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses pre-modification (determiner + adverb + adjective + noun modifier + head: a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign; noun + noun: water consumption; compound adjectives: salt-tolerant, long-term, large-scale; singular nouns as modifiers: a three-year plan); post-modification (prepositional phrases, relative clauses, -ing and -ed participle phrases, to-infinitives); combining information into one noun phrase; agreement with the head noun correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"The amount / number of… is / has…\".","options":[],"answer":"The amount of food wasted at weddings is surprisingly large.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses pre-modification (determiner + adverb + adjective + noun modifier + head: a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign; noun + noun: water consumption; compound adjectives: salt-tolerant, long-term, large-scale; singular nouns as modifiers: a three-year plan); post-modification (prepositional phrases, relative clauses, -ing and -ed participle phrases, to-infinitives); combining information into one noun phrase; agreement with the head noun correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Spin & Speak","mechanic":"spin_wheel","rules":["Work in teams of four. Player 1 spins the wheel.","Make a correct sentence about the unit theme using the cue word and pre-modification (determiner + adverb + adjective + noun modifier + head: a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign; noun + noun: water consumption; compound adjectives: salt-tolerant, long-term, large-scale; singular nouns as modifiers: a three-year plan); post-modification (prepositional phrases, relative clauses, -ing and -ed participle phrases, to-infinitives); combining information into one noun phrase; agreement with the head noun.","The team checks the sentence with the grammar box. Correct = 1 point; an extra detail = 1 bonus point.","Pass the spinner clockwise. Play for 6 minutes; the team with most points wins."],"data":[{"label":"a long-term tree-planting program"},{"label":"trees that need little water"},{"label":"water used for irrigation"},{"label":"volunteers planting seedlings"},{"label":"a plan to cut emissions"},{"label":"Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees,"},{"label":"The botanist Faisal Al-Harbi"},{"label":"one goal: restoring land"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Drip irrigation is a ___ way to water crops.","options":["highly-efficiently water-saving","highly efficient water-saving","highly efficient water-savings","water-saving highly efficient"],"answer":"highly efficient water-saving","explanation":"Pre-modification (adjectives, noun modifiers, compounds).","explanation_ar":"التعديل القبلي."},{"q":"The council approved a ___ budget for green spaces.","options":["five-millions-riyal","five-million-riyals","five million-riyals","five-million-riyal"],"answer":"five-million-riyal","explanation":"Number + singular noun modifier.","explanation_ar":"a three-year plan."},{"q":"Plastic ___ into the sea harms turtles and fish.","options":["throwing","thrown","which thrown","throws"],"answer":"thrown","explanation":"-ed participle phrase after the noun.","explanation_ar":"عبارة -ed."},{"q":"Families ___ near the mangroves have noticed more birds this year.","options":["living","who living","live","lived"],"answer":"living","explanation":"-ing participle phrase after the noun.","explanation_ar":"عبارة -ing."},{"q":"The decision ___ plastic bags was popular with shoppers.","options":["banning of","ban","to ban","to banning"],"answer":"to ban","explanation":"Relative clause, preposition or to-infinitive after the noun.","explanation_ar":"التعديل البعدي."},{"q":"","options":["Much of the food buying by households ends up in landfill.","Much of the food bought by households ends up in landfill.","Much of the food bought by households end up in landfill.","Much of the food which bought by households ends up in landfill."],"answer":"Much of the food bought by households ends up in landfill.","explanation":"Agreement with the head noun.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق مع الاسم الرئيس."},{"q":"","options":["Mangroves are salt-tolerants trees that protect coastlines.","Mangroves are salt tolerant-trees that protect coastlines.","Mangroves are salt-tolerant trees that protect coastlines.","Mangroves are trees salt-tolerant that protect coastlines."],"answer":"Mangroves are salt-tolerant trees that protect coastlines.","explanation":"Pre-modification (adjectives, noun modifiers, compounds).","explanation_ar":"التعديل القبلي."},{"q":"The quantity of fresh water ______ by farms in arid regions has led scientists to develop crops that need far less irrigation.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["consumed","consumes","which consumed","consuming"],"answer":"consumed","explanation":"-ed participle phrase after the noun.","explanation_ar":"عبارة -ed."},{"q":"A team of researchers ______ native desert plants has discovered that several species can survive for months without rain.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["studying","studies","who studying","studied"],"answer":"studying","explanation":"-ing participle phrase after the noun.","explanation_ar":"عبارة -ing."},{"q":"In \"The trees chosen for the program are native,\" what is the head noun of the subject?","options":["native","program","chosen","trees"],"answer":"trees","explanation":"Agreement with the head noun.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق مع الاسم الرئيس."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS1-IMG01","prompt":"A desert landscape being restored: rows of young native trees with drip irrigation pipes; volunteers planting seedlings; in the background, a large solar farm; on the coast, a dense band of green mangroves with birds. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A desert landscape being restored: rows of young native trees with drip irrigation pipes; volunteers planting seedlings; in the background, a large solar farm; on the coast, a dense band of green mangroves with birds"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS1-IMG02","prompt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about pre-modification (determiner + adverb + adjective + noun modifier + head: a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign; noun + noun: water consumption; compound adjectives: salt-tolerant, long-term, large-scale; singular nouns as modifiers: a three-year plan); post-modification (prepositional phrases, relative clauses, -ing and -ed participle phrases, to-infinitives); combining information into one noun phrase; agreement with the head noun, with Zaki the Falcon pointing at a blank board on the right; leave large empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about pre-modification (determiner + adverb + adjective + noun modifier + head: a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS1-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows desertification (the process by which fertile land becomes desert); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing desertification: the process by which fertile land becomes desert"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS1-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows mitigate (to make something bad less serious); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing mitigate: to make something bad less serious"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS1-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows offset (to balance one effect with an opposite one); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing offset: to balance one effect with an opposite one"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS1-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows carbon footprint (the amount of carbon dioxide produced by a person's or organization's activities); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing carbon footprint: the amount of carbon dioxide produced by a person's or organization's activities"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS1-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Spin & Speak”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Spin & Speak"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS1-W01","script":"desertification. desertification. Overgrazing has contributed to desertification.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS1-W02","script":"mitigate. mitigate. Planting trees can help mitigate climate change.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS1-W03","script":"offset. offset. Some airlines offset their carbon emissions by funding forests.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS1-W04","script":"carbon footprint. carbon footprint. Flying increases your carbon footprint.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS1-T1","script":"In 2021, Saudi Arabia launched an ambitious national program to restore its natural environment: the Saudi Green Initiative. One of its most striking goals is to plant ten billion trees across the Kingdom in the coming decades, an effort that would help rehabilitate around forty million hectares of degraded land. Planting trees in an arid country is not simple. The trees chosen for the program are mostly native species that need little water, and many are irrigated with treated wastewater from nearby cities. Along the Red Sea and Gulf coasts, volunteers carrying thousands of seedlings have planted millions of mangroves, salt-tolerant trees that protect coastlines and provide homes for fish and birds. Energy is another key area of change. The Kingdom has set a target of generating half of its electricity from renewable sources by 2030. Large-scale solar plants such as the one at Sudair can already supply power to well over a hundred thousand homes, and the country aims to reach net zero by 2060. Individuals have a role to play as well. Water consumption per person in the Kingdom is among the highest in the world, and much of the food bought by households ends up in landfill. Simple, low-cost changes such as fixing leaking taps and planning meals can make a real difference. Critics point out that large tree-planting campaigns in dry regions can fail if seedlings are not cared for after they are planted. Supporters reply that careful long-term monitoring is part of the plan. What is certain is that the choices made by this generation will shape the land inherited by the next.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS1-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “A Long-Term Tree-Planting Program That Needs Little Water: Complex Noun Phrases”. Let’s learn pre-modification (determiner + adverb + adjective + noun modifier + head: a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign; noun + noun: water consumption; compound adjectives: salt-tolerant, long-term, large-scale; singular nouns as modifiers: a three-year plan); post-modification (prepositional phrases, relative clauses, -ing and -ed participle phrases, to-infinitives); combining information into one noun phrase; agreement with the head noun together. Listen to the start of “Greening the Desert”: In 2021, Saudi Arabia launched an ambitious national program to restore its natural environment: the Saudi Green Initiative. One of its most striking goals is to plant ten billion trees across the Kingdom in the coming decades, an effort that would help rehabilitate around forty million hectares of degraded land. Planting trees in an arid country is not simple. The trees chosen for the program are mostly native species that need little water, and many are irrigated with treated wastewater from nearby cities. Look at the green words. A noun phrase has a head noun and modifiers. Pre-modifiers come before it: determiner + (adverb) + adjective(s) + noun modifier + HEAD: a remarkably successful tree-planting campaign. Nouns can modify nouns: water consumption, a solar plant, carbon emissions. Compound adjectives use hyphens: salt-tolerant, long-term, large-scale, low-cost. A number + noun modifier stays singular: a three-year plan (NOT (not: three-years)). Post-modifiers come after the head: a prepositional phrase (trees on the coast), a relative clause (trees that need little water), an -ing phrase (volunteers carrying seedlings = who are carrying), an -ed phrase (water used for irrigation = that is used), a to-infinitive (a plan to cut emissions). Long noun phrases pack information: The program plants trees. The trees are native. They need little water. → The program plants native trees that need little water. Here are some examples. A long-term tree-planting program was launched in 2021. Native species that need little water were chosen. Treated wastewater from nearby cities is used for irrigation. Be careful! Some learners say: “a three-years study.” That’s not right. We say: “a three-year study.” Number + noun modifier → singular. Quick check: \"a three-years plan\" or \"a three-year plan\"? … Think! … *a three-year plan*. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"a long-term tree-planting program\".","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS2","title":"Mangroves, Salt-Tolerant Trees, Protect the Coast: Appositives","objectives":["Students will be able to add information with appositives at the beginning, middle and end of a sentence.","Students will be able to distinguish restrictive and non-restrictive appositives.","I can write \"Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast.\"","I can write \"The botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies desert plants.\" without commas."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من إضافة المعلومات بالبدل في أول الجملة ووسطها وآخرها.","سيتمكن الطلاب من التمييز بين البدل المقيِّد وغير المقيِّد.","أستطيع أن أكتب Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast.","أستطيع أن أكتب The botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies desert plants. دون فواصل."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"circular economy","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɝkjəlɚ ɪˈkɑnəmi/","definition":"an economy in which products are reused, repaired and recycled instead of thrown away","arabic":"الاقتصاد الدائري","example":"A circular economy reduces waste.","image_id":"GV-L11-U5-LS2-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS2-W01"},{"word":"landfill","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈlændˌfɪl/","definition":"a place where waste is buried in the ground","arabic":"مكبّ النفايات","example":"Most plastic ends up in landfill.","image_id":"GV-L11-U5-LS2-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS2-W02"},{"word":"pollutant","pos":"noun","ipa":"/pəˈlutənt/","definition":"a substance that makes air, water or soil dirty and harmful","arabic":"مُلوِّث","example":"Car exhaust contains dangerous pollutants.","image_id":"GV-L11-U5-LS2-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS2-W03"},{"word":"net zero","pos":"noun","ipa":"/nɛt ˈzɪroʊ/","definition":"a state in which no more greenhouse gas is added to the atmosphere than is removed","arabic":"الحياد الصفري","example":"The Kingdom aims to reach net zero by 2060.","image_id":"GV-L11-U5-LS2-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS2-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"An appositive is a noun phrase that renames or explains the noun next to it. Non-restrictive appositives give extra information and are separated by commas (or dashes / parentheses): Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast. Al-Ahsa, the largest oasis in the Kingdom, produces millions of dates. If you remove the appositive, the sentence still identifies the noun.\nRestrictive appositives are necessary to identify the noun, so they take no commas: The botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies desert plants. (which botanist? – Faisal) My brother Saad volunteers at the reserve. (I have more than one brother.) Compare: Faisal Al-Harbi, a botanist, studies desert plants. (the name already identifies him → commas)\nAppositives can begin a sentence: A keen environmentalist, Lina started a recycling club. Or end it, often after a colon or dash: The project has one goal: restoring degraded land. / one goal – restoring degraded land. Use namely or that is for clarity: two threats, namely overgrazing and drought. The verb agrees with the main subject: Mangroves, a type of tree, grow in salty water.\nnon-restrictive (extra) → commas, dashes or parentheses\nrestrictive (needed to identify) → no commas (the botanist Faisal Al-Harbi)\nat the start: A keen environmentalist, Lina… • at the end: …one goal: restoring land\nthe verb agrees with the main subject","examples":["Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast.","Jazan, a province on the Red Sea, grows mangoes and coffee.","The botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies desert plants.","A keen environmentalist, Lina started a recycling club.","The project has one goal: restoring degraded land.","Two threats, namely overgrazing and drought, damage pastureland.","Our guide – a marine biologist from Jazan – explained the reef's importance.","Mangroves, a type of tree, grow in salty water."],"common_errors":["✗ Mangroves salt-tolerant trees protect the coast. → ✓ Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast. (Non-restrictive → commas.)","✗ The botanist, Faisal Al-Harbi, studies desert plants. (one of many botanists) → ✓ The botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies desert plants. (Restrictive → no commas.)","✗ Al-Ahsa, the largest oasis in the Kingdom produces dates. → ✓ Al-Ahsa, the largest oasis in the Kingdom, produces dates. (Close the pair.)","✗ Mangroves, a type of tree, grows in salty water. → ✓ Mangroves, a type of tree, grow in salty water. (Agree with Mangroves.)"],"arabic":"البدل شبه جملة اسمية تعيد تسمية الاسم المجاور أو تشرحه.\nالبدل غير المقيِّد يضيف معلومة إضافية ويُفصل بفاصلتين أو شَرطتين أو قوسين: Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast.\nالبدل المقيِّد ضروري لتحديد الاسم فلا يُفصل بفواصل: The botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies desert plants.\nقد يأتي البدل في أول الجملة (A keen environmentalist, Lina…) أو آخرها بعد نقطتين أو شَرطة، والفعل يتفق مع الفاعل الرئيس."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Mangroves, Salt-Tolerant Trees, Protect the Coast: Appositives”. Let’s learn appositives (a noun phrase that renames another noun): non-restrictive appositives with commas, dashes or parentheses (Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect…); restrictive appositives without commas (the botanist Faisal Al-Harbi; my brother Saad); appositives at the beginning (A keen environmentalist, Lina…) and end of a sentence (…one goal: restoring land / …one goal – restoring land); appositives with namely, that is; agreement is with the main subject, not the appositive together.","on_screen":"GV-L11-U5-LS2 | Mangroves, Salt-Tolerant Trees, Protect the Coast: Appositives","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Remember these patterns: Tabuk, a city in the northwest, is known for its farms. The scientist Dr. Huda did not attend the conference. Have you met our guide, the botanist from Jazan?","on_screen":"Tabuk, a city in the northwest, is known for its farms. | The scientist Dr. Huda did not attend the conference. | Have you met our guide, the botanist from Jazan?","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"An appositive is a noun phrase that renames or explains the noun next to it. Non-restrictive appositives give extra information and are separated by commas (or dashes / parentheses): Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast. Al-Ahsa, the largest oasis in the Kingdom, produces millions of dates. If you remove the appositive, the sentence still identifies the noun. Restrictive appositives are necessary to identify the noun, so they take no commas: The botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies desert plants. (which botanist? – Faisal) My brother Saad volunteers at the reserve. (I have more than one brother.) Compare: Faisal Al-Harbi, a botanist, studies desert plants. (the name already identifies him → commas)","on_screen":"non-restrictive (extra) → commas, dashes or parentheses • restrictive (needed to identify) → no commas (the botanist Faisal Al-Harbi) • at the start: A keen environmentalist, Lina… • at the end: …one goal: restoring land","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast. Jazan, a province on the Red Sea, grows mangoes and coffee. The botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies desert plants.","on_screen":"Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast. / Jazan, a province on the Red Sea, grows mangoes and coffee. / The botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies desert plants.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Mangroves salt-tolerant trees protect the coast.” That’s not right. We say: “Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast.” Non-restrictive → commas.","on_screen":"✗ Mangroves salt-tolerant trees protect the coast.   ✓ Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"The botanist Faisal Al-Harbi\" – why no commas? … Think! … The name identifies which botanist. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: appositives (a noun phrase that renames another noun): non-restrictive appositives with commas, dashes or parentheses (Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect…); restrictive appositives without commas (the botanist Faisal Al-Harbi; my brother Saad); appositives at the beginning (A keen environmentalist, Lina…) and end of a sentence (…one goal: restoring land / …one goal – restoring land); appositives with namely, that is; agreement is with the main subject, not the appositive.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","البدل شبه جملة اسمية تعيد تسمية الاسم المجاور أو تشرحه.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Mangroves salt-tolerant trees protect the coast. — الصحيح: Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"","options":["Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast.","Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees protect the coast,","Mangroves salt-tolerant trees protect the coast.","Mangroves salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast."],"answer":"Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast.","explanation":"Non-restrictive appositive (commas).","explanation_ar":"البدل غير المقيِّد."},{"q":"","options":["The botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies desert plants.","The botanist Faisal Al-Harbi, studies desert plants.","The botanist, Faisal Al-Harbi studies desert plants.","The, botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies desert plants."],"answer":"The botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies desert plants.","explanation":"Restrictive appositive (no commas).","explanation_ar":"البدل المقيِّد."},{"q":"___, Lina started a recycling club at her school.","options":["Is a keen environmentalist","She a keen environmentalist","Being a keen environmentalist is","A keen environmentalist"],"answer":"A keen environmentalist","explanation":"Appositive at the start of a sentence.","explanation_ar":"البدل في أول الجملة."},{"q":"The project has one main goal ___ restoring degraded land.","options":[", and",";",":","that"],"answer":":","explanation":"Appositive at the end (colon / dash / namely).","explanation_ar":"البدل في آخر الجملة."},{"q":"Mangroves, a type of tree, ___ in salty water.","options":["has grown","grow","is growing","grows"],"answer":"grow","explanation":"Agreement with the main subject.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق."},{"q":"Al-Ahsa, ______ produces millions of dates every year and was named a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2018.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["the largest oasis in the Kingdom","the largest oasis, in the Kingdom","the largest oasis in the Kingdom –","the largest oasis in the Kingdom,"],"answer":"the largest oasis in the Kingdom,","explanation":"Matching pairs of commas or dashes.","explanation_ar":"الفاصلتان المتطابقتان."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS2-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Combine with an appositive.","items":[{"q":"The Farasan Islands are a protected area.  +  They are home to rare gazelles. (appositive)","options":[],"answer":"The Farasan Islands, a protected area, are home to rare gazelles.","explanation":"Non-restrictive appositive (commas).","explanation_ar":"البدل غير المقيِّد."},{"q":"Lina is a keen photographer.  +  She documented the mangrove project. (begin with the appositive)","options":[],"answer":"A keen photographer, Lina documented the mangrove project.","explanation":"Appositive at the start of a sentence.","explanation_ar":"البدل في أول الجملة."},{"q":"The reserve faces two threats.  +  They are overgrazing and drought. (namely)","options":[],"answer":"The reserve faces two threats, namely overgrazing and drought.","explanation":"Appositive at the end (colon / dash / namely).","explanation_ar":"البدل في آخر الجملة."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS2-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the punctuation or agreement.","items":[{"q":"Al-Ahsa, the largest oasis in the Kingdom produces millions of dates.","options":[],"answer":"Al-Ahsa, the largest oasis in the Kingdom, produces millions of dates.","explanation":"Kingdom produces → Kingdom, produces. Matching pairs of commas or dashes.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Kingdom produces → Kingdom, produces. الفاصلتان المتطابقتان."},{"q":"The marine biologist, Dr. Huda, gave a talk to our class. (There are many marine biologists.)","options":[],"answer":"The marine biologist Dr. Huda gave a talk to our class. (There are many marine biologists.)","explanation":"biologist, Dr. Huda, gave → biologist Dr. Huda gave. Restrictive appositive (no commas).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: biologist, Dr. Huda, gave → biologist Dr. Huda gave. البدل المقيِّد."},{"q":"Camels, the symbol of the desert, drinks very little water.","options":[],"answer":"Camels, the symbol of the desert, drink very little water.","explanation":"drinks → drink. Agreement with the main subject.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: drinks → drink. الاتفاق."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS2-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Describe people and places with appositives.","items":[{"q":"Describe a place with an appositive in the middle.","options":[],"answer":"Abha, a city in the Asir mountains, is cool in summer.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses appositives (a noun phrase that renames another noun): non-restrictive appositives with commas, dashes or parentheses (Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect…); restrictive appositives without commas (the botanist Faisal Al-Harbi; my brother Saad); appositives at the beginning (A keen environmentalist, Lina…) and end of a sentence (…one goal: restoring land / …one goal – restoring land); appositives with namely, that is; agreement is with the main subject, not the appositive correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Introduce a person with a restrictive appositive.","options":[],"answer":"My cousin Rakan works at a solar plant.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses appositives (a noun phrase that renames another noun): non-restrictive appositives with commas, dashes or parentheses (Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect…); restrictive appositives without commas (the botanist Faisal Al-Harbi; my brother Saad); appositives at the beginning (A keen environmentalist, Lina…) and end of a sentence (…one goal: restoring land / …one goal – restoring land); appositives with namely, that is; agreement is with the main subject, not the appositive correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Begin a sentence with an appositive about a person you admire.","options":[],"answer":"A dedicated teacher, Ms. Huda started our school's garden.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses appositives (a noun phrase that renames another noun): non-restrictive appositives with commas, dashes or parentheses (Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect…); restrictive appositives without commas (the botanist Faisal Al-Harbi; my brother Saad); appositives at the beginning (A keen environmentalist, Lina…) and end of a sentence (…one goal: restoring land / …one goal – restoring land); appositives with namely, that is; agreement is with the main subject, not the appositive correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"End a sentence with a colon + appositive.","options":[],"answer":"The campaign had a simple message: waste less water.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses appositives (a noun phrase that renames another noun): non-restrictive appositives with commas, dashes or parentheses (Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect…); restrictive appositives without commas (the botanist Faisal Al-Harbi; my brother Saad); appositives at the beginning (A keen environmentalist, Lina…) and end of a sentence (…one goal: restoring land / …one goal – restoring land); appositives with namely, that is; agreement is with the main subject, not the appositive correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Use an appositive with \"namely\".","options":[],"answer":"Two habits waste the most water, namely long showers and leaking taps.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses appositives (a noun phrase that renames another noun): non-restrictive appositives with commas, dashes or parentheses (Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect…); restrictive appositives without commas (the botanist Faisal Al-Harbi; my brother Saad); appositives at the beginning (A keen environmentalist, Lina…) and end of a sentence (…one goal: restoring land / …one goal – restoring land); appositives with namely, that is; agreement is with the main subject, not the appositive correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS2-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A nature-reserve brochure.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 sentences introducing a reserve and its guide with appositives.","options":[],"answer":"The Farasan Islands Marine Sanctuary, a protected area in the Red Sea, is home to dolphins and turtles. Our guide, a marine biologist from Jazan, has worked there for ten years.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses appositives (a noun phrase that renames another noun): non-restrictive appositives with commas, dashes or parentheses (Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect…); restrictive appositives without commas (the botanist Faisal Al-Harbi; my brother Saad); appositives at the beginning (A keen environmentalist, Lina…) and end of a sentence (…one goal: restoring land / …one goal – restoring land); appositives with namely, that is; agreement is with the main subject, not the appositive correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with an appositive inside dashes.","options":[],"answer":"The reef – one of the healthiest in the region – attracts divers from many countries.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses appositives (a noun phrase that renames another noun): non-restrictive appositives with commas, dashes or parentheses (Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect…); restrictive appositives without commas (the botanist Faisal Al-Harbi; my brother Saad); appositives at the beginning (A keen environmentalist, Lina…) and end of a sentence (…one goal: restoring land / …one goal – restoring land); appositives with namely, that is; agreement is with the main subject, not the appositive correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence beginning with an appositive.","options":[],"answer":"A haven for migratory birds, the island welcomes thousands of visitors every winter.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses appositives (a noun phrase that renames another noun): non-restrictive appositives with commas, dashes or parentheses (Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect…); restrictive appositives without commas (the botanist Faisal Al-Harbi; my brother Saad); appositives at the beginning (A keen environmentalist, Lina…) and end of a sentence (…one goal: restoring land / …one goal – restoring land); appositives with namely, that is; agreement is with the main subject, not the appositive correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Tic-Tac-Grammar","mechanic":"tic_tac_toe","rules":["Two teams: X and O. Team X chooses a square.","Using the cue, the team says a correct sentence with appositives (a noun phrase that renames another noun): non-restrictive appositives with commas, dashes or parentheses (Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect…); restrictive appositives without commas (the botanist Faisal Al-Harbi; my brother Saad); appositives at the beginning (A keen environmentalist, Lina…) and end of a sentence (…one goal: restoring land / …one goal – restoring land); appositives with namely, that is; agreement is with the main subject, not the appositive (the form in brackets: +, – or ?).","If the class and teacher agree it is correct, the team marks the square.","Three squares in a row wins. Play a second round with the grid turned around."],"data":[{"cue":"program / long-term / plant trees (pre-modification)"},{"cue":"trees / need little water (relative clause)"},{"cue":"water / use / for irrigation (-ed phrase)"},{"cue":"volunteers / plant / seedlings (-ing phrase)"},{"cue":"plan / cut emissions (to-infinitive)"},{"cue":"mangroves / salt-tolerant trees (appositive)"},{"cue":"the botanist / Faisal Al-Harbi (restrictive appositive)"},{"cue":"the project / one goal / restore land (colon)"},{"cue":"a three-year study / of desert plants"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"","options":["Riyadh, the capital of Saudi Arabia is planting millions of trees.","Riyadh, the capital of Saudi Arabia, is planting millions of trees.","Riyadh the capital of Saudi Arabia, is planting millions of trees.","Riyadh the capital, of Saudi Arabia is planting millions of trees."],"answer":"Riyadh, the capital of Saudi Arabia, is planting millions of trees.","explanation":"Non-restrictive appositive (commas).","explanation_ar":"البدل غير المقيِّد."},{"q":"","options":["My brother Saad, volunteers at the reserve. (I have three brothers.)","My brother Saad volunteers at the reserve. (I have three brothers.)","My brother, Saad, volunteers at the reserve. (I have three brothers.)","My, brother Saad volunteers at the reserve. (I have three brothers.)"],"answer":"My brother Saad volunteers at the reserve. (I have three brothers.)","explanation":"Restrictive appositive (no commas).","explanation_ar":"البدل المقيِّد."},{"q":"___, the Arabian oryx was saved from extinction by a breeding program.","options":["Once extinct in the wild","It once extinct in the wild","Was once extinct in the wild","Once it was extinct in the wild it"],"answer":"Once extinct in the wild","explanation":"Appositive at the start of a sentence.","explanation_ar":"البدل في أول الجملة."},{"q":"The volunteers had one aim ___ cleaning the beach before the turtles nested.","options":["and",";","which","–"],"answer":"–","explanation":"Appositive at the end (colon / dash / namely).","explanation_ar":"البدل في آخر الجملة."},{"q":"Our guide – a marine biologist from Jazan ___ explained why coral reefs matter.","options":[":",";",",","–"],"answer":"–","explanation":"Matching pairs of commas or dashes.","explanation_ar":"الفاصلتان المتطابقتان."},{"q":"The Arabian oryx, a desert antelope, ___ survive for weeks without drinking.","options":["cans","can to","can","are able"],"answer":"can","explanation":"Agreement with the main subject.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق."},{"q":"Coral reefs face several threats, ___ rising temperatures, pollution and overfishing.","options":["namely","that","such that","which are"],"answer":"namely","explanation":"Appositive at the end (colon / dash / namely).","explanation_ar":"البدل في آخر الجملة."},{"q":"The scientist ______ led the team that counted the birds on the islands last winter.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":[", Hamad Al-Shehri",", Hamad Al-Shehri,","Hamad Al-Shehri","Hamad Al-Shehri,"],"answer":"Hamad Al-Shehri","explanation":"Restrictive appositive (no commas).","explanation_ar":"البدل المقيِّد."},{"q":"The Arabian leopard ______ is one of the most endangered big cats in the world, with only a small number left in the wild.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["– a shy, nocturnal predator,",", a shy, nocturnal predator –","– a shy, nocturnal predator –","a shy, nocturnal predator,"],"answer":"– a shy, nocturnal predator –","explanation":"Matching pairs of commas or dashes.","explanation_ar":"الفاصلتان المتطابقتان."},{"q":"In \"Mangroves, a type of tree, grow in salty water,\" what is the appositive?","options":["a type of tree","salty water","Mangroves","grow in salty water"],"answer":"a type of tree","explanation":"Non-restrictive appositive (commas).","explanation_ar":"البدل غير المقيِّد."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS2-IMG01","prompt":"A field trip to a mangrove forest on the Red Sea coast: a guide with a name badge \"Faisal Al-Harbi – Botanist\"; students in kayaks; an information board: \"Mangroves – salt-tolerant trees – protect our coast\". Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A field trip to a mangrove forest on the Red Sea coast: a guide with a name badge \"Faisal Al-Harbi – Botanist\"; students in kayaks; an information board: \"Mangroves – salt-tolerant trees – protect our coast\""},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS2-IMG02","prompt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of appositives (a noun phrase that renames another noun): non-restrictive appositives with commas, dashes or parentheses (Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect…); restrictive appositives without commas (the botanist Faisal Al-Harbi; my brother Saad); appositives at the beginning (A keen environmentalist, Lina…) and end of a sentence (…one goal: restoring land / …one goal – restoring land); appositives with namely, that is; agreement is with the main subject, not the appositive: a green tick panel and a soft red cross panel with simple objects from the theme “Environment and Responsibility”; empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of appositives (a noun phrase that renames another noun): non-restrictive appositives with commas, dashes or parentheses (Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect…)"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS2-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows circular economy (an economy in which products are reused, repaired and recycled instead of thrown away); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing circular economy: an economy in which products are reused, repaired and recycled instead of thrown away"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS2-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows landfill (a place where waste is buried in the ground); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing landfill: a place where waste is buried in the ground"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS2-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows pollutant (a substance that makes air, water or soil dirty and harmful); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing pollutant: a substance that makes air, water or soil dirty and harmful"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS2-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows net zero (a state in which no more greenhouse gas is added to the atmosphere than is removed); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing net zero: a state in which no more greenhouse gas is added to the atmosphere than is removed"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS2-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Tic-Tac-Grammar”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Tic-Tac-Grammar"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS2-W01","script":"circular economy. circular economy. A circular economy reduces waste.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS2-W02","script":"landfill. landfill. Most plastic ends up in landfill.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS2-W03","script":"pollutant. pollutant. Car exhaust contains dangerous pollutants.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS2-W04","script":"net zero. net zero. The Kingdom aims to reach net zero by 2060.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS2-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Mangroves, Salt-Tolerant Trees, Protect the Coast: Appositives”. Let’s learn appositives (a noun phrase that renames another noun): non-restrictive appositives with commas, dashes or parentheses (Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect…); restrictive appositives without commas (the botanist Faisal Al-Harbi; my brother Saad); appositives at the beginning (A keen environmentalist, Lina…) and end of a sentence (…one goal: restoring land / …one goal – restoring land); appositives with namely, that is; agreement is with the main subject, not the appositive together. Remember these patterns: Tabuk, a city in the northwest, is known for its farms. The scientist Dr. Huda did not attend the conference. Have you met our guide, the botanist from Jazan? An appositive is a noun phrase that renames or explains the noun next to it. Non-restrictive appositives give extra information and are separated by commas (or dashes / parentheses): Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast. Al-Ahsa, the largest oasis in the Kingdom, produces millions of dates. If you remove the appositive, the sentence still identifies the noun. Restrictive appositives are necessary to identify the noun, so they take no commas: The botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies desert plants. (which botanist? – Faisal) My brother Saad volunteers at the reserve. (I have more than one brother.) Compare: Faisal Al-Harbi, a botanist, studies desert plants. (the name already identifies him → commas) Here are some examples. Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast. Jazan, a province on the Red Sea, grows mangoes and coffee. The botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies desert plants. Be careful! Some learners say: “Mangroves salt-tolerant trees protect the coast.” That’s not right. We say: “Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast.” Non-restrictive → commas. Quick check: \"The botanist Faisal Al-Harbi\" – why no commas? … Think! … The name identifies which botanist. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Mangroves, salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3","title":"Mitigate, Offset, Net Zero: The Vocabulary of Sustainability","objectives":["Students will be able to use sustainability vocabulary and collocations accurately.","Students will be able to discuss environmental responsibility with precise verbs and phrases.","I can talk about my carbon footprint and how to reduce it.","I can explain what \"net zero\" and \"a circular economy\" mean."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام مفردات الاستدامة ومتلازماتها بدقة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من مناقشة المسؤولية البيئية بأفعال وعبارات دقيقة.","أستطيع أن أتحدث عن بصمتي الكربونية وكيف أقللها.","أستطيع أن أشرح معنى net zero وa circular economy."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"afforestation","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˌfɔrəˈsteɪʃən/","definition":"planting trees on land that has not had forest before","arabic":"التشجير","example":"Afforestation projects are turning bare land green.","image_id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS3-W01"},{"word":"stewardship","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈstuɚdˌʃɪp/","definition":"the responsible care of something valuable","arabic":"الرعاية المسؤولة / الإشراف","example":"Environmental stewardship is everyone's duty.","image_id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS3-W02"},{"word":"degrade","pos":"verb","ipa":"/dɪˈɡreɪd/","definition":"to damage something so that its quality becomes worse","arabic":"يُتلف / يُدهور","example":"Overgrazing degrades pastureland.","image_id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS3-W03"},{"word":"mangrove","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmænˌɡroʊv/","definition":"a tree that grows in salty water along tropical coasts","arabic":"شجرة المانغروف (القرم)","example":"Mangroves protect the coast from storms.","image_id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS3-W04"},{"word":"arid","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈærəd/","definition":"very dry, with little rain","arabic":"قاحل / جاف","example":"Much of the Arabian Peninsula is arid.","image_id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-W05","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS3-W05"},{"word":"irrigation","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɪrəˈɡeɪʃən/","definition":"supplying water to land or crops through pipes or channels","arabic":"الري","example":"Drip irrigation saves a lot of water.","image_id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-W06","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS3-W06"},{"word":"native","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈneɪtɪv/","definition":"growing or living naturally in a particular place","arabic":"محلي / أصيل","example":"Native trees need less water than imported ones.","image_id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-W07","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS3-W07"}],"explanation":{"text":"Land and water: arid (very dry), desertification (land becoming desert), degrade (damage land), afforestation (planting new forests), native species (naturally growing in a place), irrigation (watering crops), mangrove (a coastal tree in salty water). Waste and pollution: landfill, pollutant, circular economy (reuse, repair, recycle).\nClimate: carbon footprint (the CO₂ your activities produce), mitigate (make less serious), offset (balance one effect with another), net zero (no more greenhouse gas added than removed), stewardship (responsible care). Collocations: combat / halt desertification; mitigate climate change; offset carbon emissions; reduce your carbon footprint; achieve / reach net zero; divert waste from landfill; degraded land; drip irrigation; environmental stewardship.\nResponsibility language: take responsibility for; be accountable for; commit to + noun / -ing (The company committed to cutting emissions.); pledge to + verb (The city pledged to plant a million trees.); play a role in; it is incumbent on (very formal = it is the duty of). Note the difference: commit to + -ing but pledge to + base verb.\nCollocations/phrases: combat desertification • mitigate climate change • offset carbon emissions • reduce your carbon footprint | achieve net zero • divert waste from landfill • restore degraded land • plant native species | drip irrigation • a circular economy • environmental stewardship • harmful pollutants | take responsibility for • commit to reducing • pledge to plant • play a role in","examples":["Planting native trees helps combat desertification.","Drip irrigation delivers water directly to the roots.","You can reduce your carbon footprint by walking instead of driving.","The airline offsets its emissions by funding afforestation projects.","The Kingdom aims to achieve net zero by 2060.","Recycling diverts waste from landfill.","Overgrazing degrades pastureland.","The company committed to cutting its emissions by half."],"common_errors":["✗ The city pledged to planting a million trees. → ✓ The city pledged to plant a million trees. (pledge to + base verb.)","✗ The company committed to cut emissions. → ✓ The company committed to cutting emissions. (commit to + -ing.)","✗ We should decrease our carbon foot. → ✓ We should reduce our carbon footprint. (carbon footprint.)","✗ The land was degradated by overgrazing. → ✓ The land was degraded by overgrazing. (degrade → degraded.)"],"arabic":"كلمات الأرض والماء: arid (قاحل) وdesertification (التصحر) وdegrade (يُتلف) وafforestation (التشجير) وnative (محلي) وirrigation (الري) وmangrove (القرم).\nكلمات المناخ: carbon footprint (البصمة الكربونية) وmitigate (يخفّف) وoffset (يعوّض) وnet zero (الحياد الصفري) وstewardship (الرعاية المسؤولة).\nمتلازمات: combat desertification وmitigate climate change وreduce your carbon footprint وachieve net zero وdivert waste from landfill.\ncommit to + -ing لكن pledge to + المصدر: committed to cutting / pledged to plant."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Mitigate, Offset, Net Zero: The Vocabulary of Sustainability”. Let’s learn sustainability vocabulary (desertification, mitigate, offset, carbon footprint, circular economy, landfill, pollutant, net zero, afforestation, stewardship, degrade, mangrove, arid, irrigation, native); collocations (combat desertification, mitigate climate change, offset emissions, reduce your carbon footprint, achieve net zero, divert waste from landfill, degraded land, native species, drip irrigation, environmental stewardship); verbs for responsibility (take responsibility for, be accountable for, commit to, pledge to) together.","on_screen":"GV-L11-U5-LS3 | Mitigate, Offset, Net Zero: The Vocabulary of Sustainability","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Meet some new words. afforestation: planting trees on land that has not had forest before. stewardship: the responsible care of something valuable. degrade: to damage something so that its quality becomes worse.","on_screen":"afforestation | stewardship | degrade","visual":"Each word card flips over to show its picture (vocabulary image briefs) and Arabic meaning."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Land and water: arid (very dry), desertification (land becoming desert), degrade (damage land), afforestation (planting new forests), native species (naturally growing in a place), irrigation (watering crops), mangrove (a coastal tree in salty water). Waste and pollution: landfill, pollutant, circular economy (reuse, repair, recycle). Climate: carbon footprint (the CO₂ your activities produce), mitigate (make less serious), offset (balance one effect with another), net zero (no more greenhouse gas added than removed), stewardship (responsible care). Collocations: combat / halt desertification; mitigate climate change; offset carbon emissions; reduce your carbon footprint; achieve / reach net zero; divert waste from landfill; degraded land; drip irrigation; environmental stewardship.","on_screen":"sustainability vocabulary (desertification, mitigate, offset, carbon footprint, circular economy, landfill, pollutant, net zero, afforestation, stewardship, degrade, mangrove, arid, irrigation, native); collocations (combat desertification, mitigate climate change, offset emissions, reduce your carbon footprint, achieve net zero, divert waste from landfill, degraded land, native species, drip irrigation, environmental stewardship); verbs for responsibility (take responsibility for, be accountable for, commit to, pledge to)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Planting native trees helps combat desertification. Drip irrigation delivers water directly to the roots. You can reduce your carbon footprint by walking instead of driving.","on_screen":"Planting native trees helps combat desertification. / Drip irrigation delivers water directly to the roots. / You can reduce your carbon footprint by walking instead of driving.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The city pledged to planting a million trees.” That’s not right. We say: “The city pledged to plant a million trees.” pledge to + base verb.","on_screen":"✗ The city pledged to planting a million trees.   ✓ The city pledged to plant a million trees.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: What does \"net zero\" mean? … Think! … No more greenhouse gas is added than is removed. Great job! Now you can say: I can talk about my carbon footprint and how to reduce it.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can talk about my carbon footprint and how to reduce it.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: sustainability vocabulary (desertification, mitigate, offset, carbon footprint, circular economy, landfill, pollutant, net zero, afforestation, stewardship, degrade, mangrove, arid, irrigation, native); collocations (combat desertification, mitigate climate change, offset emissions, reduce your carbon footprint, achieve net zero, divert waste from landfill, degraded land, native species, drip irrigation, environmental stewardship); verbs for responsibility (take responsibility for, be accountable for, commit to, pledge to).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","كلمات الأرض والماء: arid (قاحل) وdesertification (التصحر) وdegrade (يُتلف) وafforestation (التشجير) وnative (محلي) وirrigation (الري) وmangrove (القرم).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The city pledged to planting a million trees. — الصحيح: The city pledged to plant a million trees.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أتحدث عن بصمتي الكربونية وكيف أقللها."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","items":[{"q":"Planting native trees helps ___ desertification.","options":["commit","combat","offset","divert"],"answer":"combat","explanation":"Sustainability collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"Drip ___ delivers water directly to the roots of plants.","options":["landfill","irrigation","stewardship","afforestation"],"answer":"irrigation","explanation":"Sustainability word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"You can reduce your carbon ___ by walking instead of driving.","options":["footprint","print","footstep","foot"],"answer":"footprint","explanation":"Sustainability collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"The city pledged ___ a million trees by 2030.","options":["planting","plant","to planting","to plant"],"answer":"to plant","explanation":"Responsibility language (commit to, pledge to).","explanation_ar":"لغة المسؤولية."},{"q":"Recycling diverts waste ___ landfill.","options":["out","to","from","at"],"answer":"from","explanation":"Sustainability collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"Because the region receives less than 100 millimeters of rain a year, scientists describe it as ______, and only plants adapted to drought can survive there.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["arid","degraded","circular","native"],"answer":"arid","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-B1","type":"match","instructions":"Guided practice: Match the word to its meaning.","items":[{"q":"desertification","options":["the responsible care of something valuable","an economy where products are reused, repaired and recycled","to balance one effect with an opposite one","a substance that makes air, water or soil harmful","the process by which fertile land becomes desert","planting trees where there was no forest before"],"answer":"the process by which fertile land becomes desert","explanation":"desertification → the process by which fertile land becomes desert. Sustainability word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"offset","options":["the responsible care of something valuable","an economy where products are reused, repaired and recycled","to balance one effect with an opposite one","a substance that makes air, water or soil harmful","the process by which fertile land becomes desert","planting trees where there was no forest before"],"answer":"to balance one effect with an opposite one","explanation":"offset → to balance one effect with an opposite one. Sustainability word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"circular economy","options":["the responsible care of something valuable","an economy where products are reused, repaired and recycled","to balance one effect with an opposite one","a substance that makes air, water or soil harmful","the process by which fertile land becomes desert","planting trees where there was no forest before"],"answer":"an economy where products are reused, repaired and recycled","explanation":"circular economy → an economy where products are reused, repaired and recycled. Sustainability word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"afforestation","options":["the responsible care of something valuable","an economy where products are reused, repaired and recycled","to balance one effect with an opposite one","a substance that makes air, water or soil harmful","the process by which fertile land becomes desert","planting trees where there was no forest before"],"answer":"planting trees where there was no forest before","explanation":"afforestation → planting trees where there was no forest before. Sustainability word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"stewardship","options":["the responsible care of something valuable","an economy where products are reused, repaired and recycled","to balance one effect with an opposite one","a substance that makes air, water or soil harmful","the process by which fertile land becomes desert","planting trees where there was no forest before"],"answer":"the responsible care of something valuable","explanation":"stewardship → the responsible care of something valuable. Sustainability word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"pollutant","options":["the responsible care of something valuable","an economy where products are reused, repaired and recycled","to balance one effect with an opposite one","a substance that makes air, water or soil harmful","the process by which fertile land becomes desert","planting trees where there was no forest before"],"answer":"a substance that makes air, water or soil harmful","explanation":"pollutant → a substance that makes air, water or soil harmful. Sustainability word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Talk about sustainability.","items":[{"q":"How can your family reduce its carbon footprint?","options":[],"answer":"We can share car journeys and turn off lights we aren't using.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses sustainability vocabulary (desertification, mitigate, offset, carbon footprint, circular economy, landfill, pollutant, net zero, afforestation, stewardship, degrade, mangrove, arid, irrigation, native); collocations (combat desertification, mitigate climate change, offset emissions, reduce your carbon footprint, achieve net zero, divert waste from landfill, degraded land, native species, drip irrigation, environmental stewardship); verbs for responsibility (take responsibility for, be accountable for, commit to, pledge to) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Why are native plants better for arid regions?","options":[],"answer":"Native plants need less water because they are adapted to the climate.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses sustainability vocabulary (desertification, mitigate, offset, carbon footprint, circular economy, landfill, pollutant, net zero, afforestation, stewardship, degrade, mangrove, arid, irrigation, native); collocations (combat desertification, mitigate climate change, offset emissions, reduce your carbon footprint, achieve net zero, divert waste from landfill, degraded land, native species, drip irrigation, environmental stewardship); verbs for responsibility (take responsibility for, be accountable for, commit to, pledge to) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Give an example of a circular economy in everyday life.","options":[],"answer":"Repairing old phones instead of buying new ones is part of a circular economy.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses sustainability vocabulary (desertification, mitigate, offset, carbon footprint, circular economy, landfill, pollutant, net zero, afforestation, stewardship, degrade, mangrove, arid, irrigation, native); collocations (combat desertification, mitigate climate change, offset emissions, reduce your carbon footprint, achieve net zero, divert waste from landfill, degraded land, native species, drip irrigation, environmental stewardship); verbs for responsibility (take responsibility for, be accountable for, commit to, pledge to) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"mitigate\".","options":[],"answer":"Green roofs can mitigate the heat in cities.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses sustainability vocabulary (desertification, mitigate, offset, carbon footprint, circular economy, landfill, pollutant, net zero, afforestation, stewardship, degrade, mangrove, arid, irrigation, native); collocations (combat desertification, mitigate climate change, offset emissions, reduce your carbon footprint, achieve net zero, divert waste from landfill, degraded land, native species, drip irrigation, environmental stewardship); verbs for responsibility (take responsibility for, be accountable for, commit to, pledge to) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a pledge with \"pledge to\" or \"commit to\".","options":[],"answer":"Our class pledges to stop using plastic bottles.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses sustainability vocabulary (desertification, mitigate, offset, carbon footprint, circular economy, landfill, pollutant, net zero, afforestation, stewardship, degrade, mangrove, arid, irrigation, native); collocations (combat desertification, mitigate climate change, offset emissions, reduce your carbon footprint, achieve net zero, divert waste from landfill, degraded land, native species, drip irrigation, environmental stewardship); verbs for responsibility (take responsibility for, be accountable for, commit to, pledge to) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A sustainability pledge.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 sentences of a school sustainability pledge using 3 collocations.","options":[],"answer":"We commit to reducing our carbon footprint. We pledge to plant native species in the school garden. We will divert as much waste as possible from landfill.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses sustainability vocabulary (desertification, mitigate, offset, carbon footprint, circular economy, landfill, pollutant, net zero, afforestation, stewardship, degrade, mangrove, arid, irrigation, native); collocations (combat desertification, mitigate climate change, offset emissions, reduce your carbon footprint, achieve net zero, divert waste from landfill, degraded land, native species, drip irrigation, environmental stewardship); verbs for responsibility (take responsibility for, be accountable for, commit to, pledge to) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences explaining net zero to a younger student.","options":[],"answer":"Net zero means we don't add more harmful gas to the air than we take away. Trees and clean energy help us get there.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses sustainability vocabulary (desertification, mitigate, offset, carbon footprint, circular economy, landfill, pollutant, net zero, afforestation, stewardship, degrade, mangrove, arid, irrigation, native); collocations (combat desertification, mitigate climate change, offset emissions, reduce your carbon footprint, achieve net zero, divert waste from landfill, degraded land, native species, drip irrigation, environmental stewardship); verbs for responsibility (take responsibility for, be accountable for, commit to, pledge to) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"take responsibility for\".","options":[],"answer":"Every citizen should take responsibility for the waste they produce.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses sustainability vocabulary (desertification, mitigate, offset, carbon footprint, circular economy, landfill, pollutant, net zero, afforestation, stewardship, degrade, mangrove, arid, irrigation, native); collocations (combat desertification, mitigate climate change, offset emissions, reduce your carbon footprint, achieve net zero, divert waste from landfill, degraded land, native species, drip irrigation, environmental stewardship); verbs for responsibility (take responsibility for, be accountable for, commit to, pledge to) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Memory Match","mechanic":"matching","rules":["Place all cards face down in a grid.","Turn over two cards. If the word matches its definition, keep the pair and say a sentence with the word.","If not, turn them back over; the next player plays.","The player with the most pairs wins."],"data":[{"word":"desertification","match":"the process by which fertile land becomes desert","arabic":"التصحّر"},{"word":"mitigate","match":"to make something bad less serious","arabic":"يخفّف من"},{"word":"offset","match":"to balance one effect with an opposite one","arabic":"يعوّض / يوازن"},{"word":"carbon footprint","match":"the amount of carbon dioxide produced by a person's or organization's activities","arabic":"البصمة الكربونية"},{"word":"circular economy","match":"an economy in which products are reused, repaired and recycled instead of thrown away","arabic":"الاقتصاد الدائري"},{"word":"landfill","match":"a place where waste is buried in the ground","arabic":"مكبّ النفايات"},{"word":"pollutant","match":"a substance that makes air, water or soil dirty and harmful","arabic":"مُلوِّث"},{"word":"net zero","match":"a state in which no more greenhouse gas is added to the atmosphere than is removed","arabic":"الحياد الصفري"},{"word":"afforestation","match":"planting trees on land that has not had forest before","arabic":"التشجير"},{"word":"stewardship","match":"the responsible care of something valuable","arabic":"الرعاية المسؤولة / الإشراف"},{"word":"degrade","match":"to damage something so that its quality becomes worse","arabic":"يُتلف / يُدهور"},{"word":"mangrove","match":"a tree that grows in salty water along tropical coasts","arabic":"شجرة المانغروف (القرم)"},{"word":"arid","match":"very dry, with little rain","arabic":"قاحل / جاف"},{"word":"irrigation","match":"supplying water to land or crops through pipes or channels","arabic":"الري"},{"word":"native","match":"growing or living naturally in a particular place","arabic":"محلي / أصيل"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Overgrazing by goats has ___ much of the pastureland.","options":["offset","degraded","diverted","mitigated"],"answer":"degraded","explanation":"Sustainability word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Some airlines ___ their emissions by funding forests.","options":["combat","degrade","divert","offset"],"answer":"offset","explanation":"Sustainability word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The Kingdom aims to ___ net zero by 2060.","options":["divert","achieve","combat","degrade"],"answer":"achieve","explanation":"Sustainability collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"Car exhaust contains harmful ___.","options":["pollutants","irrigations","mangroves","landfills"],"answer":"pollutants","explanation":"Sustainability word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The company has committed to ___ all single-use plastic.","options":["be eliminating","eliminate","eliminated","eliminating"],"answer":"eliminating","explanation":"Responsibility language (commit to, pledge to).","explanation_ar":"لغة المسؤولية."},{"q":"___ protect the coast from storms and provide homes for fish.","options":["Landfills","Pollutants","Footprints","Mangroves"],"answer":"Mangroves","explanation":"Sustainability word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Green roofs and shaded streets can ___ the effects of extreme heat in cities.","options":["mitigate","offset by","commit","degrade"],"answer":"mitigate","explanation":"Sustainability word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Environmental ___ means caring for nature so that future generations can enjoy it.","options":["pollutant","landfill","stewardship","desertification"],"answer":"stewardship","explanation":"Sustainability word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Instead of importing thirsty ornamental trees, the city now plants ______ species such as the ghaf and the acacia, which are adapted to the local climate.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["circular","polluted","native","degraded"],"answer":"native","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"The new regulation requires restaurants to compost their food waste, which will ______ thousands of tons of material from landfill every year.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["offset","divert","degrade","pledge"],"answer":"divert","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-IMG01","prompt":"A sustainability fair: posters \"Reduce Your Carbon Footprint\", \"Circular Economy: Repair – Reuse – Recycle\", \"Combat Desertification\"; a model of drip irrigation; a student calculating her carbon footprint on a tablet. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A sustainability fair: posters \"Reduce Your Carbon Footprint\", \"Circular Economy: Repair – Reuse – Recycle\", \"Combat Desertification\"; a model of drip irrigation; a student calculating her carbon footprint on a tablet"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-IMG02","prompt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words; empty labels. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows afforestation (planting trees on land that has not had forest before); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing afforestation: planting trees on land that has not had forest before"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows stewardship (the responsible care of something valuable); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing stewardship: the responsible care of something valuable"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows degrade (to damage something so that its quality becomes worse); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing degrade: to damage something so that its quality becomes worse"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows mangrove (a tree that grows in salty water along tropical coasts); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing mangrove: a tree that grows in salty water along tropical coasts"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-W05","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows arid (very dry, with little rain); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing arid: very dry, with little rain"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-W06","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows irrigation (supplying water to land or crops through pipes or channels); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing irrigation: supplying water to land or crops through pipes or channels"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-W07","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows native (growing or living naturally in a particular place); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing native: growing or living naturally in a particular place"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS3-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Memory Match”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Memory Match"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS3-W01","script":"afforestation. afforestation. Afforestation projects are turning bare land green.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS3-W02","script":"stewardship. stewardship. Environmental stewardship is everyone's duty.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS3-W03","script":"degrade. degrade. Overgrazing degrades pastureland.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS3-W04","script":"mangrove. mangrove. Mangroves protect the coast from storms.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS3-W05","script":"arid. arid. Much of the Arabian Peninsula is arid.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS3-W06","script":"irrigation. irrigation. Drip irrigation saves a lot of water.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS3-W07","script":"native. native. Native trees need less water than imported ones.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS3-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Mitigate, Offset, Net Zero: The Vocabulary of Sustainability”. Let’s learn sustainability vocabulary (desertification, mitigate, offset, carbon footprint, circular economy, landfill, pollutant, net zero, afforestation, stewardship, degrade, mangrove, arid, irrigation, native); collocations (combat desertification, mitigate climate change, offset emissions, reduce your carbon footprint, achieve net zero, divert waste from landfill, degraded land, native species, drip irrigation, environmental stewardship); verbs for responsibility (take responsibility for, be accountable for, commit to, pledge to) together. Meet some new words. afforestation: planting trees on land that has not had forest before. stewardship: the responsible care of something valuable. degrade: to damage something so that its quality becomes worse. Land and water: arid (very dry), desertification (land becoming desert), degrade (damage land), afforestation (planting new forests), native species (naturally growing in a place), irrigation (watering crops), mangrove (a coastal tree in salty water). Waste and pollution: landfill, pollutant, circular economy (reuse, repair, recycle). Climate: carbon footprint (the CO₂ your activities produce), mitigate (make less serious), offset (balance one effect with another), net zero (no more greenhouse gas added than removed), stewardship (responsible care). Collocations: combat / halt desertification; mitigate climate change; offset carbon emissions; reduce your carbon footprint; achieve / reach net zero; divert waste from landfill; degraded land; drip irrigation; environmental stewardship. Here are some examples. Planting native trees helps combat desertification. Drip irrigation delivers water directly to the roots. You can reduce your carbon footprint by walking instead of driving. Be careful! Some learners say: “The city pledged to planting a million trees.” That’s not right. We say: “The city pledged to plant a million trees.” pledge to + base verb. Quick check: What does \"net zero\" mean? … Think! … No more greenhouse gas is added than is removed. Great job! Now you can say: I can talk about my carbon footprint and how to reduce it.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS4","title":"The Oryx – Once Extinct in the Wild – Has Returned: Punctuating Appositives (SAT)","objectives":["Students will be able to punctuate non-restrictive appositives with matching commas, dashes or parentheses.","Students will be able to use a colon only after a complete clause and avoid punctuation in restrictive appositives.","I can write \"The oryx – once extinct in the wild – has returned.\" with two matching dashes.","I can avoid a colon after \"such as\" or \"including\"."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من ترقيم البدل غير المقيِّد بفاصلتين أو شَرطتين أو قوسين متطابقين.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام النقطتين بعد جملة كاملة فقط وتجنب الترقيم في البدل المقيِّد.","أستطيع أن أكتب The oryx – once extinct in the wild – has returned. بشَرطتين متطابقتين.","أستطيع أن أتجنب النقطتين بعد such as أو including."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"desertification","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˌzɝtəfəˈkeɪʃən/","definition":"the process by which fertile land becomes desert","arabic":"التصحّر","example":"Overgrazing has contributed to desertification.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"mitigate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈmɪtəˌɡeɪt/","definition":"to make something bad less serious","arabic":"يخفّف من","example":"Planting trees can help mitigate climate change.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"offset","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɔfˈsɛt/","definition":"to balance one effect with an opposite one","arabic":"يعوّض / يوازن","example":"Some airlines offset their carbon emissions by funding forests.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"carbon footprint","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkɑrbən ˈfʊtˌprɪnt/","definition":"the amount of carbon dioxide produced by a person's or organization's activities","arabic":"البصمة الكربونية","example":"Flying increases your carbon footprint.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"circular economy","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɝkjəlɚ ɪˈkɑnəmi/","definition":"an economy in which products are reused, repaired and recycled instead of thrown away","arabic":"الاقتصاد الدائري","example":"A circular economy reduces waste.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"landfill","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈlændˌfɪl/","definition":"a place where waste is buried in the ground","arabic":"مكبّ النفايات","example":"Most plastic ends up in landfill.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"A non-restrictive appositive or extra phrase must be enclosed by a matching pair: two commas, two dashes or parentheses. ✔ The oryx, once extinct in the wild, has returned. ✔ The oryx – once extinct in the wild – has returned. ✘ (not: The oryx – once extinct in the wild, has returned.) (mixed pair). If the appositive ends the sentence, use one comma or one dash before it.\nA colon can introduce an appositive, a list or an explanation, but only after a complete independent clause: ✔ The reserve protects three species: the oryx, the gazelle and the ibex. ✘ (not: The reserve protects: the oryx…) (no complete clause before it). ✘ (not: species such as: the oryx). No colon after such as, including, are, include.\nRestrictive appositives and titles before names take no punctuation: the Saudi botanist Faisal Al-Harbi; the novel Cities of Salt; the zoologist Dr. Huda Al-Ghamdi. SAT tip: look for the first punctuation mark around an extra phrase and match the second one to it. Then check whether the sentence still works if the extra phrase is removed.\nmatching pairs: , … , • – … – • ( … ) – never mix\ncolon only after a complete clause • no colon after such as / including / are\nrestrictive appositive / title + name → no punctuation\nremove the extra phrase → the sentence must still work","examples":["The oryx, once extinct in the wild, has returned to Saudi reserves.","The gazelle – a symbol of grace in Arabic poetry – lives in the reserve.","The reef (one of the healthiest in the region) attracts divers.","The reserve protects three species: the oryx, the gazelle and the ibex.","The reserve protects species such as the oryx and the gazelle.","The Saudi botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies native plants.","The campaign had one message – waste less water.","Abha, a city in the Asir mountains, is surrounded by juniper forests."],"common_errors":["✗ The oryx – once extinct in the wild, has returned. → ✓ The oryx – once extinct in the wild – has returned. (Matching pair.)","✗ The reserve protects: the oryx, the gazelle and the ibex. → ✓ The reserve protects the oryx, the gazelle and the ibex. (No colon after a verb.)","✗ Species such as: the oryx and the gazelle live there. → ✓ Species such as the oryx and the gazelle live there. (No colon after such as.)","✗ The Saudi botanist, Faisal Al-Harbi, studies native plants. (one of many botanists) → ✓ The Saudi botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies native plants. (Restrictive → no commas.)"],"arabic":"البدل غير المقيِّد يُحاط بزوج متطابق: فاصلتان أو شَرطتان أو قوسان، ولا نخلط بين الفاصلة والشَّرطة.\nالنقطتان تأتيان بعد جملة مستقلة كاملة فقط، ولا تأتيان بعد such as أو including أو بعد الفعل مباشرة.\nالبدل المقيِّد واللقب قبل الاسم لا يأخذان ترقيمًا: the Saudi botanist Faisal Al-Harbi.\nنصيحة SAT: طابق علامة الترقيم الثانية مع الأولى، وتأكد أن الجملة تبقى صحيحة إذا حذفت العبارة الإضافية."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Oryx – Once Extinct in the Wild – Has Returned: Punctuating Appositives (SAT)”. Let’s learn SAT boundary questions with appositives and supplementary elements: matching pairs (two commas or two dashes, not one of each); parentheses; a colon only after a complete independent clause (no colon after such as, including or a verb); a dash before an appositive at the end of a sentence; no punctuation around restrictive appositives or between a title and a name (the Saudi botanist Faisal Al-Harbi) together.","on_screen":"GV-L11-U5-LS4 | The Oryx – Once Extinct in the Wild – Has Returned: Punctuating Appositives (SAT)","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"A non-restrictive appositive or extra phrase must be enclosed by a matching pair: two commas, two dashes or parentheses. ✔ The oryx, once extinct in the wild, has returned. ✔ The oryx – once extinct in the wild – has returned. ✘ (not: The oryx – once extinct in the wild, has returned.) (mixed pair). If the appositive ends the sentence, use one comma or one dash before it.","on_screen":"SAT boundary questions with appositives and supplementary elements: matching pairs (two commas or two dashes, not one of each); parentheses; a colon only after a complete independent clause (no colon after such as, including or a verb); a dash before an appositive at the end of a sentence; no punctuation around restrictive appositives or between a title and a name (the Saudi botanist Faisal Al-Harbi)","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"A non-restrictive appositive or extra phrase must be enclosed by a matching pair: two commas, two dashes or parentheses. ✔ The oryx, once extinct in the wild, has returned. ✔ The oryx – once extinct in the wild – has returned. ✘ (not: The oryx – once extinct in the wild, has returned.) (mixed pair). If the appositive ends the sentence, use one comma or one dash before it. A colon can introduce an appositive, a list or an explanation, but only after a complete independent clause: ✔ The reserve protects three species: the oryx, the gazelle and the ibex. ✘ (not: The reserve protects: the oryx…) (no complete clause before it). ✘ (not: species such as: the oryx). No colon after such as, including, are, include.","on_screen":"matching pairs: , … , • – … – • ( … ) – never mix • colon only after a complete clause • no colon after such as / including / are • restrictive appositive / title + name → no punctuation","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The oryx, once extinct in the wild, has returned to Saudi reserves. The gazelle – a symbol of grace in Arabic poetry – lives in the reserve. The reef (one of the healthiest in the region) attracts divers.","on_screen":"The oryx, once extinct in the wild, has returned to Saudi reserves. / The gazelle – a symbol of grace in Arabic poetry – lives in the reserve. / The reef (one of the healthiest in the region) attracts divers.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The oryx – once extinct in the wild, has returned.” That’s not right. We say: “The oryx – once extinct in the wild – has returned.” Matching pair.","on_screen":"✗ The oryx – once extinct in the wild, has returned.   ✓ The oryx – once extinct in the wild – has returned.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"The oryx – once extinct in the wild, has returned.\" What's wrong? … Think! … The pair doesn't match (dash + comma). Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"The oryx – once extinct in the wild – has returned.\" with two matching dashes.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"The oryx – once extinct in the wild – has returned.\" with two matching dashes.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: SAT boundary questions with appositives and supplementary elements: matching pairs (two commas or two dashes, not one of each); parentheses; a colon only after a complete independent clause (no colon after such as, including or a verb); a dash before an appositive at the end of a sentence; no punctuation around restrictive appositives or between a title and a name (the Saudi botanist Faisal Al-Harbi).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","البدل غير المقيِّد يُحاط بزوج متطابق: فاصلتان أو شَرطتان أو قوسان، ولا نخلط بين الفاصلة والشَّرطة.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The oryx – once extinct in the wild, has returned. — الصحيح: The oryx – once extinct in the wild – has returned.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب The oryx – once extinct in the wild – has returned. بشَرطتين متطابقتين."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Annual Report: The Desert Reserve (editing passage)","body":"The reserve – a protected area of 2,000 square kilometers, is home to many rare animals. This year, the team counted three species; the oryx, the gazelle and the ibex. The ecologist, Saad Al-Otaibi, led the survey. (one of several ecologists) Volunteers also planted native trees such as: acacia and ghaf. The oryx, once extinct in the wild – is now thriving.","word_count":62,"audio_id":""}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correctly punctuated answer.","items":[{"q":"The Arabian oryx ______ has returned to several protected reserves in Saudi Arabia.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["once extinct in the wild,",", once extinct in the wild –","– once extinct in the wild –","– once extinct in the wild,"],"answer":"– once extinct in the wild –","explanation":"Matching pair of commas or dashes.","explanation_ar":"الزوج المتطابق."},{"q":"","options":["The reserve protects: the oryx, the gazelle and the ibex.","The reserve protects three species, the oryx: the gazelle and the ibex.","The reserve protects three: species the oryx, the gazelle and the ibex.","The reserve protects three species: the oryx, the gazelle and the ibex."],"answer":"The reserve protects three species: the oryx, the gazelle and the ibex.","explanation":"Colon only after a complete clause.","explanation_ar":"النقطتان."},{"q":"","options":["The reserve protects species such as, the oryx and the gazelle.","The reserve protects species such as the oryx and the gazelle.","The reserve protects species such as: the oryx and the gazelle.","The reserve protects species, such as: the oryx and the gazelle."],"answer":"The reserve protects species such as the oryx and the gazelle.","explanation":"Colon only after a complete clause.","explanation_ar":"النقطتان."},{"q":"","options":["The Saudi botanist, Faisal Al-Harbi studies native plants.","The Saudi botanist – Faisal Al-Harbi, studies native plants.","The Saudi botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies native plants.","The Saudi botanist Faisal Al-Harbi, studies native plants."],"answer":"The Saudi botanist Faisal Al-Harbi studies native plants.","explanation":"No punctuation in restrictive appositives or titles.","explanation_ar":"لا ترقيم في البدل المقيِّد."},{"q":"","options":["The campaign had one simple message, – waste less water.","The campaign had one simple – message waste less water.","The campaign had: one simple message waste less water.","The campaign had one simple message – waste less water."],"answer":"The campaign had one simple message – waste less water.","explanation":"One comma or dash before a final appositive.","explanation_ar":"البدل في آخر الجملة."},{"q":"The coral reef near the island ______ attracts divers from many countries every year.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["(one of the healthiest in the region)","one of the healthiest in the region)","(one of the healthiest in the region,",", one of the healthiest in the region)"],"answer":"(one of the healthiest in the region)","explanation":"Parentheses for extra information.","explanation_ar":"الأقواس."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS4-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the punctuation.","items":[{"q":"AlUla, a region of sandstone mountains – is also a nature reserve.","options":[],"answer":"AlUla, a region of sandstone mountains, is also a nature reserve.","explanation":"mountains – is → mountains, is. Matching pair of commas or dashes.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: mountains – is → mountains, is. الزوج المتطابق."},{"q":"The project plants : native trees such as acacia and ghaf.","options":[],"answer":"The project plants native trees such as acacia and ghaf.","explanation":": native trees → native trees. Colon only after a complete clause.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: : native trees → native trees. النقطتان."},{"q":"The marine biologist, Dr. Huda Al-Ghamdi, counted the turtles. (one of several biologists)","options":[],"answer":"The marine biologist Dr. Huda Al-Ghamdi counted the turtles. (one of several biologists)","explanation":"biologist, Dr. Huda Al-Ghamdi, counted → biologist Dr. Huda Al-Ghamdi counted. No punctuation in restrictive appositives or titles.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: biologist, Dr. Huda Al-Ghamdi, counted → biologist Dr. Huda Al-Ghamdi counted. لا ترقيم في البدل المقيِّد."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS4-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the punctuation.","items":[{"q":"The volunteers had one goal; cleaning the beach before the turtles nested.","options":[],"answer":"The volunteers had one goal: cleaning the beach before the turtles nested.","explanation":"goal; cleaning → goal: cleaning. One comma or dash before a final appositive.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: goal; cleaning → goal: cleaning. البدل في آخر الجملة."},{"q":"Mangroves – salt-tolerant trees, protect the coastline.","options":[],"answer":"Mangroves – salt-tolerant trees – protect the coastline.","explanation":"trees, protect → trees – protect. Matching pair of commas or dashes.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: trees, protect → trees – protect. الزوج المتطابق."},{"q":"The reserve is home to animals including: the oryx and the ibex.","options":[],"answer":"The reserve is home to animals including the oryx and the ibex.","explanation":"including: the → including the. Colon only after a complete clause.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: including: the → including the. النقطتان."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS4-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Edit the reserve report.","items":[{"q":"The reserve – a protected area of 2,000 square kilometers, is home to many rare animals.","options":[],"answer":"The reserve – a protected area of 2,000 square kilometers – is home to many rare animals.","explanation":"kilometers, is → kilometers – is. Matching pair of commas or dashes.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: kilometers, is → kilometers – is. الزوج المتطابق."},{"q":"This year, the team counted three species; the oryx, the gazelle and the ibex.","options":[],"answer":"This year, the team counted three species: the oryx, the gazelle and the ibex.","explanation":"species; the → species: the. Matching pair of commas or dashes.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: species; the → species: the. الزوج المتطابق."},{"q":"The ecologist, Saad Al-Otaibi, led the survey. (one of several ecologists) Volunteers also planted native trees such as acacia and ghaf.","options":[],"answer":"The ecologist Saad Al-Otaibi led the survey. (one of several ecologists) Volunteers also planted native trees such as acacia and ghaf.","explanation":"ecologist, Saad Al-Otaibi, led → ecologist Saad Al-Otaibi led. Matching pair of commas or dashes.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: ecologist, Saad Al-Otaibi, led → ecologist Saad Al-Otaibi led. الزوج المتطابق."},{"q":"The ecologist Saad Al-Otaibi led the survey. (one of several ecologists) Volunteers also planted native trees such as: acacia and ghaf.","options":[],"answer":"The ecologist Saad Al-Otaibi led the survey. (one of several ecologists) Volunteers also planted native trees such as acacia and ghaf.","explanation":"as: acacia → as acacia. Matching pair of commas or dashes.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: as: acacia → as acacia. الزوج المتطابق."},{"q":"The oryx, once extinct in the wild – is now thriving.","options":[],"answer":"The oryx, once extinct in the wild, is now thriving.","explanation":"– → ,. Matching pair of commas or dashes.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح:  – → ,. الزوج المتطابق."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS4-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write correctly punctuated sentences.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence with an appositive inside two dashes.","options":[],"answer":"The ghaf tree – the national tree of the UAE – grows across the Arabian Peninsula.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses SAT boundary questions with appositives and supplementary elements: matching pairs (two commas or two dashes, not one of each); parentheses; a colon only after a complete independent clause (no colon after such as, including or a verb); a dash before an appositive at the end of a sentence; no punctuation around restrictive appositives or between a title and a name (the Saudi botanist Faisal Al-Harbi) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with a colon introducing a list after a complete clause.","options":[],"answer":"Our garden needs three things: shade, compost and patience.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses SAT boundary questions with appositives and supplementary elements: matching pairs (two commas or two dashes, not one of each); parentheses; a colon only after a complete independent clause (no colon after such as, including or a verb); a dash before an appositive at the end of a sentence; no punctuation around restrictive appositives or between a title and a name (the Saudi botanist Faisal Al-Harbi) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with a restrictive appositive (no commas).","options":[],"answer":"The environmental scientist Dr. Lina Al-Qahtani visited our school.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses SAT boundary questions with appositives and supplementary elements: matching pairs (two commas or two dashes, not one of each); parentheses; a colon only after a complete independent clause (no colon after such as, including or a verb); a dash before an appositive at the end of a sentence; no punctuation around restrictive appositives or between a title and a name (the Saudi botanist Faisal Al-Harbi) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Word Builder","mechanic":"word_builder","rules":["The teacher reads a word and a short clue.","Teams build the word with letter tiles in 30 seconds.","The team holds up the word; correct spelling = 1 point, with correct capital letter or punctuation where needed = 1 bonus point.","Continue until all words are used."],"data":[{"word":"desertification","clue":"the process by which fertile land becomes desert"},{"word":"afforestation","clue":"planting trees on land that has not had forest before"},{"word":"stewardship","clue":"the responsible care of something valuable"},{"word":"irrigation","clue":"supplying water to land or crops through pipes or channels"},{"word":"pollutant","clue":"a substance that makes air, water or soil dirty and harmful"},{"word":"mangrove","clue":"a tree that grows in salty water along tropical coasts"},{"word":"mitigate","clue":"to make something bad less serious"},{"word":"offset","clue":"to balance one effect with an opposite one"},{"word":"landfill","clue":"a place where waste is buried in the ground"},{"word":"degrade","clue":"to damage something so that its quality becomes worse"},{"word":"arid","clue":"very dry, with little rain"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"","options":["The ibex a wild mountain goat, lives in rocky areas.","The ibex, a wild mountain goat, lives in rocky areas.","The ibex – a wild mountain goat, lives in rocky areas.","The ibex, a wild mountain goat – lives in rocky areas."],"answer":"The ibex, a wild mountain goat, lives in rocky areas.","explanation":"Matching pair of commas or dashes.","explanation_ar":"الزوج المتطابق."},{"q":"","options":["The campaign has two aims: to reduce waste and to save water.","The campaign has two aims, to reduce waste: and to save water.","The campaign has: two aims to reduce waste and to save water.","The campaign has two: aims to reduce waste and to save water."],"answer":"The campaign has two aims: to reduce waste and to save water.","explanation":"Colon only after a complete clause.","explanation_ar":"النقطتان."},{"q":"","options":["The documentary Planet of Sand won an award.","The documentary Planet of Sand, won an award.","The documentary – Planet of Sand, won an award.","The documentary, Planet of Sand won an award."],"answer":"The documentary Planet of Sand won an award.","explanation":"No punctuation in restrictive appositives or titles.","explanation_ar":"لا ترقيم في البدل المقيِّد."},{"q":"","options":["The river was polluted by one main source, – a nearby factory.","The river was polluted by – one main source a nearby factory.","The river was polluted by one main source – a nearby factory.","The river was polluted by one main source a – nearby factory."],"answer":"The river was polluted by one main source – a nearby factory.","explanation":"One comma or dash before a final appositive.","explanation_ar":"البدل في آخر الجملة."},{"q":"","options":["The Farasan Islands (a marine sanctuary), are home to turtles.","The Farasan Islands, a marine sanctuary) are home to turtles.","The Farasan Islands (a marine sanctuary are home to turtles.","The Farasan Islands (a marine sanctuary) are home to turtles."],"answer":"The Farasan Islands (a marine sanctuary) are home to turtles.","explanation":"Parentheses for extra information.","explanation_ar":"الأقواس."},{"q":"The ghaf tree ______ can survive long droughts because its roots reach water deep underground.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["a native desert species,",", a native desert species –","– a native desert species,",", a native desert species,"],"answer":", a native desert species,","explanation":"Matching pair of commas or dashes.","explanation_ar":"الزوج المتطابق."},{"q":"Scientists have identified the main threats to the reef ______ rising sea temperatures, pollution from the coast and overfishing.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":[";",":",", such as:","including:"],"answer":":","explanation":"Colon only after a complete clause.","explanation_ar":"النقطتان."},{"q":"The environmental ______ spent six months recording the songs of migratory birds on the Red Sea coast.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["researcher – Noura Al-Shammari,","researcher Noura Al-Shammari,","researcher, Noura Al-Shammari","researcher Noura Al-Shammari"],"answer":"researcher Noura Al-Shammari","explanation":"No punctuation in restrictive appositives or titles.","explanation_ar":"لا ترقيم في البدل المقيِّد."},{"q":"The volunteers achieved what many had thought impossible ______ they planted ten thousand mangroves in a single week.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["and:","; such as",":",","],"answer":":","explanation":"Colon only after a complete clause.","explanation_ar":"النقطتان."},{"q":"Which pair of punctuation marks is NOT correct around an extra phrase?","options":["a dash and a comma","two parentheses","two dashes","two commas"],"answer":"a dash and a comma","explanation":"Matching pair of commas or dashes.","explanation_ar":"الزوج المتطابق."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS4-IMG01","prompt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Environment and Responsibility” that shows SAT boundary questions with appositives and supplementary elements: matching pairs (two commas or two dashes, not one of each); parentheses; a colon only after a complete independent clause (no colon after such as, including or a verb); a dash before an appositive at the end of a sentence; no punctuation around restrictive appositives or between a title and a name (the Saudi botanist Faisal Al-Harbi) in action. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Environment and Responsibility” that shows SAT boundary questions with appositives and supplementary elements: matching pairs (two commas or two dashes, not one of each); parentheses; a colon only after a complete independent clause (no colon after such as, including or a verb); a dash before an appositive at the end of a sentence; no punctuation around restrictive appositives or between a title and a name (the Saudi botanist Faisal Al-Harbi) in action"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS4-IMG02","prompt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS4-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Word Builder”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Word Builder"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS4-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Oryx – Once Extinct in the Wild – Has Returned: Punctuating Appositives (SAT)”. Let’s learn SAT boundary questions with appositives and supplementary elements: matching pairs (two commas or two dashes, not one of each); parentheses; a colon only after a complete independent clause (no colon after such as, including or a verb); a dash before an appositive at the end of a sentence; no punctuation around restrictive appositives or between a title and a name (the Saudi botanist Faisal Al-Harbi) together. A non-restrictive appositive or extra phrase must be enclosed by a matching pair: two commas, two dashes or parentheses. ✔ The oryx, once extinct in the wild, has returned. ✔ The oryx – once extinct in the wild – has returned. ✘ (not: The oryx – once extinct in the wild, has returned.) (mixed pair). If the appositive ends the sentence, use one comma or one dash before it. A non-restrictive appositive or extra phrase must be enclosed by a matching pair: two commas, two dashes or parentheses. ✔ The oryx, once extinct in the wild, has returned. ✔ The oryx – once extinct in the wild – has returned. ✘ (not: The oryx – once extinct in the wild, has returned.) (mixed pair). If the appositive ends the sentence, use one comma or one dash before it. A colon can introduce an appositive, a list or an explanation, but only after a complete independent clause: ✔ The reserve protects three species: the oryx, the gazelle and the ibex. ✘ (not: The reserve protects: the oryx…) (no complete clause before it). ✘ (not: species such as: the oryx). No colon after such as, including, are, include. Here are some examples. The oryx, once extinct in the wild, has returned to Saudi reserves. The gazelle – a symbol of grace in Arabic poetry – lives in the reserve. The reef (one of the healthiest in the region) attracts divers. Be careful! Some learners say: “The oryx – once extinct in the wild, has returned.” That’s not right. We say: “The oryx – once extinct in the wild – has returned.” Matching pair. Quick check: \"The oryx – once extinct in the wild, has returned.\" What's wrong? … Think! … The pair doesn't match (dash + comma). Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"The oryx – once extinct in the wild – has returned.\" with two matching dashes.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS5","title":"Our Green School Plan: Language in Use","objectives":["Students will be able to present an environmental action plan for their school.","Students will be able to write a formal plan with complex noun phrases, appositives and sustainability vocabulary, punctuated correctly, and answer SAT-style transition and synthesis items.","I can present a green plan for my school.","I can write a formal environmental plan."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من عرض خطة عمل بيئية لمدرستهم.","سيتمكن الطلاب من كتابة خطة رسمية بأشباه جمل اسمية معقدة وبدل ومفردات الاستدامة مع ترقيم صحيح، والإجابة عن أسئلة الروابط والتركيب بنمط SAT.","أستطيع أن أعرض خطة خضراء لمدرستي.","أستطيع أن أكتب خطة بيئية رسمية."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"desertification","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˌzɝtəfəˈkeɪʃən/","definition":"the process by which fertile land becomes desert","arabic":"التصحّر","example":"Overgrazing has contributed to desertification.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"mitigate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈmɪtəˌɡeɪt/","definition":"to make something bad less serious","arabic":"يخفّف من","example":"Planting trees can help mitigate climate change.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"offset","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɔfˈsɛt/","definition":"to balance one effect with an opposite one","arabic":"يعوّض / يوازن","example":"Some airlines offset their carbon emissions by funding forests.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"carbon footprint","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkɑrbən ˈfʊtˌprɪnt/","definition":"the amount of carbon dioxide produced by a person's or organization's activities","arabic":"البصمة الكربونية","example":"Flying increases your carbon footprint.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"circular economy","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɝkjəlɚ ɪˈkɑnəmi/","definition":"an economy in which products are reused, repaired and recycled instead of thrown away","arabic":"الاقتصاد الدائري","example":"A circular economy reduces waste.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"landfill","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈlændˌfɪl/","definition":"a place where waste is buried in the ground","arabic":"مكبّ النفايات","example":"Most plastic ends up in landfill.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"pollutant","pos":"noun","ipa":"/pəˈlutənt/","definition":"a substance that makes air, water or soil dirty and harmful","arabic":"مُلوِّث","example":"Car exhaust contains dangerous pollutants.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"net zero","pos":"noun","ipa":"/nɛt ˈzɪroʊ/","definition":"a state in which no more greenhouse gas is added to the atmosphere than is removed","arabic":"الحياد الصفري","example":"The Kingdom aims to reach net zero by 2060.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"afforestation","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˌfɔrəˈsteɪʃən/","definition":"planting trees on land that has not had forest before","arabic":"التشجير","example":"Afforestation projects are turning bare land green.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"stewardship","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈstuɚdˌʃɪp/","definition":"the responsible care of something valuable","arabic":"الرعاية المسؤولة / الإشراف","example":"Environmental stewardship is everyone's duty.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"degrade","pos":"verb","ipa":"/dɪˈɡreɪd/","definition":"to damage something so that its quality becomes worse","arabic":"يُتلف / يُدهور","example":"Overgrazing degrades pastureland.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"mangrove","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmænˌɡroʊv/","definition":"a tree that grows in salty water along tropical coasts","arabic":"شجرة المانغروف (القرم)","example":"Mangroves protect the coast from storms.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"arid","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈærəd/","definition":"very dry, with little rain","arabic":"قاحل / جاف","example":"Much of the Arabian Peninsula is arid.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"irrigation","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɪrəˈɡeɪʃən/","definition":"supplying water to land or crops through pipes or channels","arabic":"الري","example":"Drip irrigation saves a lot of water.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"native","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈneɪtɪv/","definition":"growing or living naturally in a particular place","arabic":"محلي / أصيل","example":"Native trees need less water than imported ones.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Today you use all of Unit 5: complex noun phrases, appositives, sustainability vocabulary and the punctuation of appositives.\nA formal action plan describes the current situation, proposes specific actions (often numbered: first, second, third), explains the benefits of each action and ends with the overall goal. Complex noun phrases make the plan precise; appositives add useful details without extra sentences.\n✔ solar panels installed on the roof • native species that need little water\n✔ Our school, a large building…, • the garden – currently a dusty area –\n✔ carbon footprint, native species, drip irrigation, divert from landfill, stewardship\n✔ …three categories: food, plastic and paper.","examples":["Our school, a large building with 1,200 students, uses a lot of energy.","Solar panels installed on the roof could supply electricity.","The garden – currently a dusty area – should be replanted.","Native species that need little water are best.","A drip irrigation system using recycled water would water the plants.","Waste should be sorted into three categories: food, plastic and paper.","Composting diverts food waste from landfill.","The school could become a model of environmental stewardship."],"common_errors":["✗ The garden – currently a dusty area, should be replanted. → ✓ The garden – currently a dusty area – should be replanted. (Matching pair.)","✗ Waste should be sorted into: food, plastic and paper. → ✓ Waste should be sorted into three categories: food, plastic and paper. (Colon after a complete clause.)","✗ a three-parts plan → ✓ a three-part plan (Singular noun modifier.)"],"arabic":"نراجع أشباه الجمل الاسمية المعقدة والبدل ومفردات الاستدامة وترقيم البدل.\nخطة العمل الرسمية تصف الوضع الحالي، وتقترح إجراءات محددة مرقّمة، وتشرح فوائد كل إجراء، وتنتهي بالهدف العام.\nأشباه الجمل الاسمية المعقدة تجعل الخطة دقيقة، والبدل يضيف تفاصيل مفيدة دون جمل إضافية."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Our Green School Plan: Language in Use”. Let’s learn complex noun phrases, appositives, sustainability vocabulary and collocations, punctuation of appositives; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together.","on_screen":"GV-L11-U5-LS5 | Our Green School Plan: Language in Use","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Green school challenge: in groups, write a three-point plan to make your school more sustainable (energy, water, waste). Present it to the \"school board\". Each group must use two complex noun phrases, two appositives (one with dashes), four sustainability words and one colon introducing a list.","on_screen":"Language in use","visual":"Split screen: two cast members talking, with speech bubbles appearing."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Today you use all of Unit 5: complex noun phrases, appositives, sustainability vocabulary and the punctuation of appositives. A formal action plan describes the current situation, proposes specific actions (often numbered: first, second, third), explains the benefits of each action and ends with the overall goal. Complex noun phrases make the plan precise; appositives add useful details without extra sentences.","on_screen":"✔ solar panels installed on the roof • native species that need little water • ✔ Our school, a large building…, • the garden – currently a dusty area – • ✔ carbon footprint, native species, drip irrigation, divert from landfill, stewardship","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Our school, a large building with 1,200 students, uses a lot of energy. Solar panels installed on the roof could supply electricity. The garden – currently a dusty area – should be replanted.","on_screen":"Our school, a large building with 1,200 students, uses a lot of energy. / Solar panels installed on the roof could supply electricity. / The garden – currently a dusty area – should be replanted.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The garden – currently a dusty area, should be replanted.” That’s not right. We say: “The garden – currently a dusty area – should be replanted.” Matching pair.","on_screen":"✗ The garden – currently a dusty area, should be replanted.   ✓ The garden – currently a dusty area – should be replanted.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: what is wrong with “Waste should be sorted into: food, plastic and paper.”? … Think! … It should be “Waste should be sorted into three categories: food, plastic and paper.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can present a green plan for my school.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can present a green plan for my school.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: complex noun phrases, appositives, sustainability vocabulary and collocations, punctuation of appositives; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نراجع أشباه الجمل الاسمية المعقدة والبدل ومفردات الاستدامة وترقيم البدل.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The garden – currently a dusty area, should be replanted. — الصحيح: The garden – currently a dusty area – should be replanted.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أعرض خطة خضراء لمدرستي."]},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Our Green School Plan – Ziyad","body":"Our school, a large building with more than 1,200 students, uses a great deal of energy and water.\nWe propose a three-part plan to reduce its carbon footprint.\nFirst, solar panels installed on the roof could supply much of the electricity needed during the day.\nSecond, the garden – currently a dusty area with a few imported plants – should be replaced with native species that need little water.\nA drip irrigation system using recycled water from the sinks would water the new plants.\nThird, the waste produced by the canteen should be sorted into three categories: food, plastic and paper.\nFood waste could be composted, which would divert it from landfill.\nWith this plan, our school could become a model of environmental stewardship for the whole city.","word_count":128,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS5-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS5-A1","type":"true_false","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Ziyad's plan. True or False?","items":[{"q":"Ziyad's school has more than 1,200 students.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Our Green School Plan – Ziyad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"He proposes a five-part plan.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Our Green School Plan – Ziyad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The solar panels would be installed on the roof.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Our Green School Plan – Ziyad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Ziyad wants to keep the imported plants in the garden.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Our Green School Plan – Ziyad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The irrigation system would use recycled water from the sinks.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Our Green School Plan – Ziyad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Canteen waste would be sorted into four categories.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Our Green School Plan – Ziyad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS5-B1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"We propose a ___ plan to reduce the school's carbon footprint.","options":["three-part","three-partly","three parts'","three-parts"],"answer":"three-part","explanation":"Complex noun phrase.","explanation_ar":"شبه الجملة الاسمية."},{"q":"Solar panels ___ on the roof could supply much of the electricity.","options":["which installed","installed","install","installing"],"answer":"installed","explanation":"Complex noun phrase.","explanation_ar":"شبه الجملة الاسمية."},{"q":"The garden ___ should be replaced with native species.","options":[", currently a dusty area –","– currently a dusty area –","– currently a dusty area,","currently a dusty area,"],"answer":"– currently a dusty area –","explanation":"Appositive and its punctuation.","explanation_ar":"البدل وترقيمه."},{"q":"Food waste could be composted, which would ___ it from landfill.","options":["divert","offset","mitigate","degrade"],"answer":"divert","explanation":"Sustainability vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مفردات الاستدامة."},{"q":"","options":["Waste should be sorted: into three categories food, plastic and paper.","Waste should be sorted into three categories, food: plastic and paper.","Waste should be sorted into: food, plastic and paper.","Waste should be sorted into three categories: food, plastic and paper."],"answer":"Waste should be sorted into three categories: food, plastic and paper.","explanation":"Appositive and its punctuation.","explanation_ar":"البدل وترقيمه."},{"q":"Solar panels would reduce the school's electricity bills. ______ they would teach students how renewable energy works.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["In contrast,","Nevertheless,","In addition,","However,"],"answer":"In addition,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS5-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Plan your green proposal.","items":[{"q":"Describe your school or area with an appositive: ___","options":[],"answer":"Our neighborhood, a residential area near the university, has very little shade.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses complex noun phrases, appositives, sustainability vocabulary and collocations, punctuation of appositives; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A complex noun phrase describing one action: ___","options":[],"answer":"A tree-planting project led by students and parents","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses complex noun phrases, appositives, sustainability vocabulary and collocations, punctuation of appositives; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A sentence with an appositive in dashes: ___","options":[],"answer":"The empty lot – a dusty space beside the mosque – could become a garden.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses complex noun phrases, appositives, sustainability vocabulary and collocations, punctuation of appositives; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A sentence with a colon and a list: ___","options":[],"answer":"The project needs three things: native trees, volunteers and a water supply.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses complex noun phrases, appositives, sustainability vocabulary and collocations, punctuation of appositives; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"The overall goal with a sustainability word: ___","options":[],"answer":"Our goal is to make the neighborhood a model of environmental stewardship.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses complex noun phrases, appositives, sustainability vocabulary and collocations, punctuation of appositives; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS5-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write your green plan.","items":[{"q":"Plan: current situation, three actions, benefits, goal.","options":[],"answer":"Neighborhood with no shade; plant native trees, collect recyclables, install solar street lights; cooler streets, less waste, lower bills; a greener neighborhood","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses complex noun phrases, appositives, sustainability vocabulary and collocations, punctuation of appositives; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write your green plan for your school or neighborhood (8–9 sentences).","options":[],"answer":"Our neighborhood, a residential area of about 500 homes, has almost no trees or shade. We propose a three-step plan to make it greener and cooler. First, volunteers from local schools could plant native species such as ghaf and acacia along the main streets. A drip irrigation system using treated wastewater would keep the young trees alive. Second, recycling bins – currently found only at the supermarket – should be placed on every street. Third, solar street lights installed by the municipality would reduce the area's carbon footprint. The plan has three main benefits: cooler streets, less waste in landfill and lower energy costs. With everyone's help, our neighborhood could become a model of environmental stewardship.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses complex noun phrases, appositives, sustainability vocabulary and collocations, punctuation of appositives; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Check: matching pairs of dashes? colon after a complete clause? singular noun modifiers (three-step)? Fix one mistake.","options":[],"answer":"I changed \"recycling bins – currently found only at the supermarket, should\" to \"– currently found only at the supermarket – should\".","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses complex noun phrases, appositives, sustainability vocabulary and collocations, punctuation of appositives; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Quiz Show Challenge","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["Teams take turns choosing a category and a cue word.","Grammar: make a sentence with complex noun phrases, appositives, sustainability vocabulary and collocations, punctuation of appositives; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review). Words: give the meaning. Spelling: spell it. Write It!: write a sentence with correct punctuation.","Correct answers score 10 points; teams may steal a question another team misses.","The highest score after 8 minutes wins."],"data":[{"cue":"desertification","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"mitigate","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"offset","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"carbon footprint","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"circular economy","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"landfill","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"pollutant","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"net zero","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"afforestation","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"stewardship","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"degrade","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"mangrove","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"arid","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"irrigation","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"native","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The council approved a ___ project to plant trees along the highway.","options":["ten-kilometers","ten-kilometerly","ten-kilometer","ten kilometers'"],"answer":"ten-kilometer","explanation":"Complex noun phrase.","explanation_ar":"شبه الجملة الاسمية."},{"q":"Trees ___ by students last year are now two meters tall.","options":["plant","which planted","planting","planted"],"answer":"planted","explanation":"Complex noun phrase.","explanation_ar":"شبه الجملة الاسمية."},{"q":"The Arabian oryx ___ has been reintroduced to several reserves.","options":["– a desert antelope,",", a desert antelope –",", a desert antelope,","a desert antelope,"],"answer":", a desert antelope,","explanation":"Appositive and its punctuation.","explanation_ar":"البدل وترقيمه."},{"q":"Many cities are working to ___ desertification by planting trees.","options":["combat","divert","degrade","offset"],"answer":"combat","explanation":"Sustainability vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مفردات الاستدامة."},{"q":"","options":["The volunteers: needed two things gloves and water.","The volunteers needed two things: gloves and water.","The volunteers needed: gloves and water.","The volunteers needed two things, gloves: and water."],"answer":"The volunteers needed two things: gloves and water.","explanation":"Appositive and its punctuation.","explanation_ar":"البدل وترقيمه."},{"q":"The school installed solar panels last year. ______ its electricity bills have fallen by almost a third.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Since then,","However,","Similarly,","For instance,"],"answer":"Since then,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"Some people think that recycling alone will solve the waste problem. ______ experts say that reducing and reusing are even more important.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["However,","Therefore,","For example,","As a result,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• The Saudi Green Initiative was launched in 2021.\n• It aims to plant ten billion trees in the Kingdom.\n• It also aims to protect 30% of the country's land and sea areas.\n• Native species are being used because they need less water.\n\nThe student wants to explain why native species were chosen for the initiative. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["It aims to plant ten billion trees.","It aims to protect land and sea areas.","The initiative was launched in 2021.","The Saudi Green Initiative, launched in 2021, uses native tree species because they need less water."],"answer":"The Saudi Green Initiative, launched in 2021, uses native tree species because they need less water.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Mangroves are salt-tolerant trees that grow along tropical coasts.\n• They protect coastlines from storms and erosion.\n• They provide habitats for fish and birds.\n• Volunteers have planted millions of mangroves along Saudi coasts.\n\nThe student wants to emphasize the environmental benefits of mangroves. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Saudi Arabia has long coastlines.","Mangroves, salt-tolerant coastal trees, protect shorelines from storms and erosion and provide habitats for fish and birds.","Volunteers have planted millions of mangroves.","Mangroves grow along tropical coasts."],"answer":"Mangroves, salt-tolerant coastal trees, protect shorelines from storms and erosion and provide habitats for fish and birds.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"Pick the best opening sentence for a formal green plan:","options":["Our school, a large building with more than 1,200 students – uses a great deal of energy and water.","Our school, a large building with more than 1,200 students, uses a great deal of energy and water.","Our school is: a large building with more than 1,200 students.","Our school a large building, with more than 1,200 students uses a great deal of energy and water."],"answer":"Our school, a large building with more than 1,200 students, uses a great deal of energy and water.","explanation":"Appositive and its punctuation.","explanation_ar":"البدل وترقيمه."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS5-IMG01","prompt":"A school roof with solar panels and a small vegetable garden with drip irrigation; Ziyad presenting a plan to the principal and the student council; a poster \"Our Green School Plan 2026\". Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A school roof with solar panels and a small vegetable garden with drip irrigation; Ziyad presenting a plan to the principal and the student council; a poster \"Our Green School Plan 2026\""},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS5-IMG02","prompt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Environment and Responsibility”, speech bubbles left empty. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Environment and Responsibility”, speech bubbles left empty"},{"id":"GV-L11-U5-LS5-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Quiz Show Challenge”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Quiz Show Challenge"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS5-T1","script":"Our school, a large building with more than 1,200 students, uses a great deal of energy and water. We propose a three-part plan to reduce its carbon footprint. First, solar panels installed on the roof could supply much of the electricity needed during the day. Second, the garden – currently a dusty area with a few imported plants – should be replaced with native species that need little water. A drip irrigation system using recycled water from the sinks would water the new plants. Third, the waste produced by the canteen should be sorted into three categories: food, plastic and paper. Food waste could be composted, which would divert it from landfill. With this plan, our school could become a model of environmental stewardship for the whole city.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L11-U5-LS5-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Our Green School Plan: Language in Use”. Let’s learn complex noun phrases, appositives, sustainability vocabulary and collocations, punctuation of appositives; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together. Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Green school challenge: in groups, write a three-point plan to make your school more sustainable (energy, water, waste). Present it to the \"school board\". Each group must use two complex noun phrases, two appositives (one with dashes), four sustainability words and one colon introducing a list. Today you use all of Unit 5: complex noun phrases, appositives, sustainability vocabulary and the punctuation of appositives. A formal action plan describes the current situation, proposes specific actions (often numbered: first, second, third), explains the benefits of each action and ends with the overall goal. Complex noun phrases make the plan precise; appositives add useful details without extra sentences. Here are some examples. Our school, a large building with 1,200 students, uses a lot of energy. Solar panels installed on the roof could supply electricity. The garden – currently a dusty area – should be replanted. Be careful! Some learners say: “The garden – currently a dusty area, should be replanted.” That’s not right. We say: “The garden – currently a dusty area – should be replanted.” Matching pair. Quick check: what is wrong with “Waste should be sorted into: food, plastic and paper.”? … Think! … It should be “Waste should be sorted into three categories: food, plastic and paper.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can present a green plan for my school.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"The ministry announced a ___ campaign.","options":["nation-wide-tree planting","nationwide tree-planting","nationwides tree-planting","tree-planting nationwide"],"answer":"nationwide tree-planting","explanation":"Pre-modification (adjectives, noun modifiers, compounds).","explanation_ar":"التعديل القبلي."},{"q":"Scientists completed a ___ survey of the coral reef.","options":["five years","five-yearly","five-year","five-years"],"answer":"five-year","explanation":"Number + singular noun modifier.","explanation_ar":"a three-year plan."},{"q":"The water ___ for irrigation comes from treated wastewater.","options":["using","used","uses","which used"],"answer":"used","explanation":"-ed participle phrase after the noun.","explanation_ar":"عبارة -ed."},{"q":"Volunteers ___ along the beach collected two tons of plastic.","options":["work","working","worked","who working"],"answer":"working","explanation":"-ing participle phrase after the noun.","explanation_ar":"عبارة -ing."},{"q":"The number of turtles nesting on the island ___ increased.","options":["were","have","are","has"],"answer":"has","explanation":"Agreement with the head noun.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق مع الاسم الرئيس."},{"q":"The government has introduced ______ that will require all new buildings to collect rainwater.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["a set of strict water-saving rules","a strict set water-saving of rules","water-saving a set of strict rules","a set of water-saving strict rule"],"answer":"a set of strict water-saving rules","explanation":"Pre-modification (adjectives, noun modifiers, compounds).","explanation_ar":"التعديل القبلي."},{"q":"","options":["Riyadh, the capital of the Kingdom is planting millions of trees.","Riyadh, the capital, of the Kingdom is planting millions of trees.","Riyadh the capital of the Kingdom, is planting millions of trees.","Riyadh, the capital of the Kingdom, is planting millions of trees."],"answer":"Riyadh, the capital of the Kingdom, is planting millions of trees.","explanation":"Non-restrictive appositive (commas).","explanation_ar":"البدل غير المقيِّد."},{"q":"","options":["The zoologist, Dr. Saad Al-Malki studies desert foxes. (one of many zoologists)","The zoologist Dr. Saad Al-Malki, studies desert foxes. (one of many zoologists)","The zoologist Dr. Saad Al-Malki studies desert foxes. (one of many zoologists)","The zoologist, Dr. Saad Al-Malki, studies desert foxes. (one of many zoologists)"],"answer":"The zoologist Dr. Saad Al-Malki studies desert foxes. (one of many zoologists)","explanation":"Restrictive appositive (no commas).","explanation_ar":"البدل المقيِّد."},{"q":"___, Lina organized a beach clean-up every month.","options":["She a passionate environmentalist","A passionate environmentalist","Being a passionate environmentalist is","Was a passionate environmentalist"],"answer":"A passionate environmentalist","explanation":"Appositive at the start of a sentence.","explanation_ar":"البدل في أول الجملة."},{"q":"Desert foxes, small nocturnal animals, ___ during the cooler hours of the night.","options":["has hunted","hunts","hunt","is hunting"],"answer":"hunt","explanation":"Agreement with the main subject.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق."},{"q":"The Asir Mountains ______ receive more rainfall than any other region of the Kingdom.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["– a range along the southwestern coast –","a range along the southwestern coast,","– a range along the southwestern coast,",", a range along the southwestern coast –"],"answer":"– a range along the southwestern coast –","explanation":"Matching pairs of commas or dashes.","explanation_ar":"الفاصلتان المتطابقتان."},{"q":"Farmers are using windbreaks and native shrubs to ___ desertification.","options":["divert","degrade","offset","combat"],"answer":"combat","explanation":"Sustainability collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"Many countries have promised to ___ net zero by the middle of the century.","options":["degrade","divert","achieve","combat"],"answer":"achieve","explanation":"Sustainability collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"You can reduce your carbon ___ by using public transportation.","options":["foot","footstep","print","footprint"],"answer":"footprint","explanation":"Sustainability collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"Overgrazing over many decades has ______ much of the region's pastureland, leaving bare soil that is easily blown away by the wind.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["offset","degraded","diverted","mitigated"],"answer":"degraded","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"The company pledged ___ its plastic packaging by 2030.","options":["to eliminating","eliminating","to eliminate","eliminate"],"answer":"to eliminate","explanation":"Responsibility language (commit to, pledge to).","explanation_ar":"لغة المسؤولية."},{"q":"","options":["The project has three goals: saving water, planting trees and reducing waste.","The project's goals are: saving water, planting trees and reducing waste.","The project has: three goals saving water, planting trees and reducing waste.","The project has three goals, saving water: planting trees and reducing waste."],"answer":"The project has three goals: saving water, planting trees and reducing waste.","explanation":"Colon only after a complete clause.","explanation_ar":"النقطتان."},{"q":"","options":["The reserve protects animals, such as: the oryx and the ibex.","The reserve protects: animals such as the oryx and the ibex.","The reserve protects animals such as: the oryx and the ibex.","The reserve protects animals such as the oryx and the ibex."],"answer":"The reserve protects animals such as the oryx and the ibex.","explanation":"Colon only after a complete clause.","explanation_ar":"النقطتان."},{"q":"The new solar plant ______ provides clean electricity to thousands of homes in the region.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":[", one of the largest in the Middle East)","one of the largest in the Middle East)","(one of the largest in the Middle East)","(one of the largest in the Middle East,"],"answer":"(one of the largest in the Middle East)","explanation":"Parentheses for extra information.","explanation_ar":"الأقواس."},{"q":"Many people assume that deserts contain very little life. ______ the Arabian desert is home to hundreds of species of plants, insects, reptiles and mammals.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["In fact,","For this reason,","Therefore,","Similarly,"],"answer":"In fact,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• The Arabian oryx is a desert antelope.\n• It became extinct in the wild in the 1970s.\n• Breeding programs in zoos kept the species alive.\n• Oryx have since been released into protected reserves in Saudi Arabia.\n\nThe student wants to emphasize how the oryx was saved. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["The Arabian oryx is a desert antelope.","Many animals live in zoos.","Saudi Arabia has protected reserves.","After the Arabian oryx became extinct in the wild in the 1970s, breeding programs kept the species alive until it could be released into protected reserves."],"answer":"After the Arabian oryx became extinct in the wild in the 1970s, breeding programs kept the species alive until it could be released into protected reserves.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"How can we fix it? \"the mangroves – salt-tolerant trees, protects the coast from: storms and erosion.\"","options":["The mangroves – salt-tolerant trees, protect the coast from storms and erosion.","The mangroves – salt-tolerant trees – protect the coast from storms and erosion.","the mangroves – salt-tolerant trees – protect the coast from storms and erosion.","The mangroves – salt-tolerant trees – protects the coast from: storms and erosion."],"answer":"The mangroves – salt-tolerant trees – protect the coast from storms and erosion.","explanation":"Matching pairs of commas or dashes.","explanation_ar":"الفاصلتان المتطابقتان."},{"q":"In \"The seedlings planted by the students last year need daily watering,\" what is the head noun?","options":["seedlings","year","watering","students"],"answer":"seedlings","explanation":"Agreement with the head noun.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق مع الاسم الرئيس."},{"q":"Which sentence contains a restrictive appositive?","options":["My friend Hamad volunteers at the reserve.","Hamad, my friend, volunteers at the reserve.","Hamad – my friend – volunteers at the reserve.","Hamad (my friend) volunteers at the reserve."],"answer":"My friend Hamad volunteers at the reserve.","explanation":"Restrictive appositive (no commas).","explanation_ar":"البدل المقيِّد."}],"theme_ar":"البيئة والمسؤولية","essential_question_ar":"مَن المسؤول عن حماية البيئة: الحكومات أم الشركات أم الأفراد؟","can_do":"I can build complex noun phrases with pre- and post-modification, use appositives to add information, use sustainability vocabulary and collocations, and punctuate appositives correctly with commas, dashes and colons.","can_do_ar":"أستطيع أن أبني أشباه جمل اسمية معقدة بالتعديل القبلي والبعدي، وأستخدم البدل لإضافة المعلومات، وأستخدم مفردات الاستدامة ومتلازماتها، وأرقّم البدل بشكل صحيح بالفواصل والشَّرطات والنقطتين."});
